1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,860
Listen.

2
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,060
Practice.

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:09,930
Speak.

4
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:12,350
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:17,620
Essential business English for, well, for the workplace today.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,440
It's not just about lists of words.

7
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:21,500
It's about giving you that practical edge.

8
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:22,090
Exactly.

9
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:27,350
A toolkit, really, to help you navigate your professional life with more confidence, you know, more precision.

10
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:27,750
Yeah.

11
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:28,270
And impact.

12
00:00:28,310 --> 00:00:28,510
Right.

13
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:37,010
We're highlighting the surprising facts, maybe, and the phrases that genuinely make a difference right now in your work life.

14
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:41,130
Yeah, not just knowing the words, but knowing how and when to deploy them effectively.

15
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:41,830
Exactly.

16
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:50,270
We'll break down common situations, give you vocabulary that actually sticks and useful phrases you can literally start using, you know, after you finish listening.

17
00:00:50,910 --> 00:00:55,070
By the end, you should have a much clearer path to leveling up your professional English.

18
00:00:55,230 --> 00:00:55,630
That's good.

19
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:00,460
So as we sort of kick this off, what's an area that often stands out to you?

20
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:04,650
Where do professionals sometimes stumble, even if their English is generally pretty good?

21
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:05,980
That's a great starting point.

22
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:10,120
And honestly, it often comes down to the verbs people choose, especially around teams.

23
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,010
You can accidentally undersell yourself or misunderstand the dynamic just by picking the wrong word.

24
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:23,200
So let's maybe start there with core verbs for team dynamics, because some verbs are just foundational when you talk about teams.

25
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,000
Makes sense.

26
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:24,400
Okay.

27
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:25,660
So think about managing a team.

28
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,480
You often hear to run a team or to lead a team.

29
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,110
It sounds similar, right?

30
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:32,850
Both mean you're in charge.

31
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:35,160
Yeah, basically the same meaning on the surface.

32
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:36,360
But there's a subtle difference.

33
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:43,190
Like if you say, I run a small team of software developers, it often sounds more operational, day-to-day, getting things done.

34
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:44,840
Okay, focused on the mechanics.

35
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:45,260
Exactly.

36
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:56,450
But if you say, I lead a small team of software developers, that tends to suggest something more, more strategic, maybe more inspirational, setting direction, motivating people.

37
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:57,960
That's a really good distinction.

38
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:00,060
Run feels more like management.

39
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:04,810
Lead feels more like, well, leadership, vision, empowerment.

40
00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:05,530
Precisely.

41
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:06,990
And think about an interview.

42
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:12,260
Choosing lead consciously signals you're thinking bigger picture, not just ticking off tasks.

43
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:12,640
Right.

44
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:15,220
It frames how you view your contribution.

45
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:17,990
Beyond just managing, there's also creating a team.

46
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:21,010
Yes, to build the team, which is exactly what it sounds like.

47
00:02:21,610 --> 00:02:23,620
Creating a new team, maybe from scratch.

48
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:25,000
Yeah, putting the pieces together.

49
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:28,450
And it emphasizes the strategic side, doesn't it?

50
00:02:28,730 --> 00:02:31,070
Picking the right people, thinking about how they'll work together.

51
00:02:31,410 --> 00:02:35,400
Like the first challenge for the new CEO is to build a new senior management team.

52
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:36,860
It's not just filling slots.

53
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:38,880
It's designing the engine, basically.

54
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:40,680
Requires real foresight.

55
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:41,080
Yeah.

56
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,730
And then once a team exists, people move in and out.

57
00:02:45,110 --> 00:02:46,530
So you have to join a team.

58
00:02:46,790 --> 00:02:48,610
Becoming part of an existing group.

59
00:02:48,690 --> 00:02:48,890
Right.

60
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:49,890
Simple enough.

61
00:02:50,470 --> 00:02:52,960
To join a team means to become part of a team.

62
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:58,720
But it highlights that whole initial integration phase, you know, getting used to the culture, the dynamics.

63
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:00,760
Yeah, fitting in and the opposite.

64
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:01,840
To leave a team.

65
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:03,100
Departing.

66
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,890
Could be for a promotion, moving departments, leaving the company altogether.

67
00:03:07,290 --> 00:03:11,340
Like, Susan is moving to a different department next month, so she will be leaving the team.

68
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:14,940
Okay, so those verbs cover the basic life cycle of team membership and management.

69
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:17,530
Run, lead, build, join, leave.

70
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:18,050
Got it.

71
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:18,670
Exactly.

72
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:19,940
Foundational stuff.

73
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:26,010
Okay, so moving on from the verbs, how we describe the quality of a team is also really important.

74
00:03:26,370 --> 00:03:27,680
And that's where adjectives come in.

75
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:29,500
They give you a quick snapshot.

76
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,310
Right, like shorthand for effectiveness.

77
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:32,010
Precisely.

78
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:33,570
Take successful team.

79
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:34,700
Pretty straightforward.

80
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:37,150
A team that achieves its goals gets the job done.

81
00:03:37,910 --> 00:03:40,270
Our sales team was really successful last quarter.

82
00:03:40,410 --> 00:03:40,670
Okay.

83
00:03:41,010 --> 00:03:42,330
Positive outcome focused.

84
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:42,630
Yeah.

85
00:03:43,230 --> 00:03:47,600
And similar, but maybe with a bit more of a competitive feel, is winning team.

86
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:53,810
Often implies they're succeeding against rivals or hitting top metrics, like a sports team almost.

87
00:03:53,930 --> 00:03:56,880
Winning implies beating someone or something else maybe?

88
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:59,720
Often, yeah, or just being at the absolute top.

89
00:04:00,310 --> 00:04:01,810
Then you have experience team.

90
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:03,290
This one's really valuable.

91
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:04,850
Because experience counts for a lot.

92
00:04:05,030 --> 00:04:05,410
It does.

93
00:04:05,910 --> 00:04:09,860
It means the members have significant relevant history in their roles or industry.

94
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:14,270
For instance, most of the people in our group have been working in the industry for years.

95
00:04:14,310 --> 00:04:15,930
It's a very experienced team.

96
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:18,900
And why is that so valuable, apart from the obvious?

97
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:22,310
Well, an experienced team often anticipates problems better.

98
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:23,770
They've seen things before.

99
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:27,420
They might avoid those early teething problems we'll talk about later.

100
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:29,380
They can often solve issues faster.

101
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:29,820
Right.

102
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:33,500
It implies competence, reliability, practical knowledge.

103
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:35,420
They've learned the hard way sometimes.

104
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:36,040
Exactly.

105
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:38,350
It's shorthand for they know what they're doing.

106
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:39,310
OK, that makes sense.

107
00:04:39,690 --> 00:04:42,990
So we've got verbs for actions, adjectives for quality.

108
00:04:43,710 --> 00:04:45,540
What about the concepts around teams?

109
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:46,980
Good transition.

110
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:51,310
Let's talk about key team concepts, important nouns.

111
00:04:51,870 --> 00:04:55,480
These words capture the dynamics and how people function within the team.

112
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:56,680
Like team effort.

113
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:57,680
That's a common one.

114
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,370
Very common and important.

115
00:04:59,630 --> 00:05:03,190
It means people collaborated, worked together towards a goal.

116
00:05:03,410 --> 00:05:05,340
It stresses the collective achievement.

117
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:05,660
Right.

118
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:07,160
Like I didn't do this alone.

119
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:08,440
It was a real team effort.

120
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:11,120
Avoid sounding like you're taking all the credit.

121
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:11,880
Precisely.

122
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:13,760
Fosters that sense of shared success.

123
00:05:14,230 --> 00:05:15,470
Then there's team player.

124
00:05:16,130 --> 00:05:16,590
Ah, yes.

125
00:05:17,270 --> 00:05:18,080
Highly valued.

126
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:21,370
Someone who works well with others puts the group first.

127
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:21,860
That's it.

128
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:28,770
If someone says he's not really a team player, prefers working alone, that can be a real red flag in many workplaces.

129
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:29,350
Yeah.

130
00:05:29,370 --> 00:05:31,160
Implies they might hinder collaboration.

131
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:34,000
Then you have team spirit, that feeling.

132
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:34,360
Yeah.

133
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,590
The vibe, enthusiasm, cooperation, camaraderie.

134
00:05:37,610 --> 00:05:38,110
Exactly.

135
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:39,150
That positive energy.

136
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:40,130
I love working here.

137
00:05:40,170 --> 00:05:41,830
There's such a great sense of team spirit.

138
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:46,120
It makes a huge difference to the work environment, morale, everything.

139
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,980
And often productivity, too, I bet.

140
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:48,720
Absolutely.

141
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:50,680
And activity is designed to boost that.

142
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,690
That's team building.

143
00:05:52,170 --> 00:05:55,990
It can be workshops, off-sites, anything to improve cohesion.

144
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:56,430
Right.

145
00:05:56,530 --> 00:05:58,860
And guiding the whole thing is the team leader.

146
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:01,780
The person in charge of guiding, motivating.

147
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:04,200
Paul is one of our best team leaders.

148
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:05,940
Means he's good at that role.

149
00:06:06,630 --> 00:06:09,710
These nouns really help define how a team operates.

150
00:06:10,070 --> 00:06:11,130
They really do.

151
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:13,950
Okay, so how do we use this in everyday chat?

152
00:06:14,150 --> 00:06:15,230
Right, let's make it practical.

153
00:06:15,770 --> 00:06:19,030
There are some essential phrases for team collaboration you'll hear all the time.

154
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:20,880
They're really smooth interactions.

155
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:22,040
Like, to touch base.

156
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:23,060
Exactly.

157
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:26,490
Means having a quick chat, getting an update, checking in.

158
00:06:27,030 --> 00:06:31,250
It's efficient, professional, but less formal than scheduling a proper meeting.

159
00:06:31,730 --> 00:06:35,080
So, the boss wants to touch base with you tomorrow about the report.

160
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:36,580
Just a quick sync up.

161
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:37,260
Pretty much.

162
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,690
Then there's to keep someone posted.

163
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:41,050
Meaning keep them updated.

164
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:44,150
Yeah, regularly inform them about progress or news.

165
00:06:44,290 --> 00:06:47,170
Super important for transparency, especially in projects.

166
00:06:47,710 --> 00:06:51,160
Don't worry, I'll keep you posted on how the client negotiation is going.

167
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:53,400
Sets the expectation for communication.

168
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:55,320
Avoids surprises.

169
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:55,890
Right.

170
00:06:56,550 --> 00:07:01,590
And a personal favorite for collaboration, to bounce ideas off someone.

171
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:02,560
Oh yeah, I use that one.

172
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:04,780
Discussing ideas to get feedback.

173
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:05,760
Like a sounding board.

174
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:06,360
Precisely.

175
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:10,610
It's collaborative, shows you value their opinion, and often sparks better ideas.

176
00:07:10,910 --> 00:07:11,770
Hey, got five minutes.

177
00:07:11,790 --> 00:07:13,870
I'd like to bounce some ideas off you for the presentation.

178
00:07:13,990 --> 00:07:16,270
Turns a solo effort into a joint one, almost.

179
00:07:16,830 --> 00:07:18,040
Low-pressure brainstorming.

180
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:19,080
Exactly.

181
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:20,560
These aren't just polite words.

182
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:23,730
They're tools for making teamwork actually work smoothly.

183
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:27,490
Okay, that's a great foundation for team talk.

184
00:07:27,510 --> 00:07:28,710
Now, shifting gears a bit.

185
00:07:29,510 --> 00:07:29,990
Meetings.

186
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:33,180
The unavoidable reality of most workplaces.

187
00:07:33,290 --> 00:07:34,180
Ah, yes.

188
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:35,700
Mastering the meeting.

189
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:37,110
A critical skill.

190
00:07:37,830 --> 00:07:39,550
And the language you use matters a lot.

191
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:41,060
Especially the formality, right?

192
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:45,730
Let's get into the core meeting verbs and those formal and informal choices.

193
00:07:45,810 --> 00:07:46,420
Good idea.

194
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:49,960
Knowing when to use which can really affect perception.

195
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:52,160
So being present at a meeting.

196
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:54,930
You can attend a meeting or go to a meeting.

197
00:07:55,090 --> 00:07:55,570
Exactly.

198
00:07:55,690 --> 00:07:56,910
Attend is more formal.

199
00:07:57,430 --> 00:08:00,940
The CEO requires all department heads to attend the strategy meeting.

200
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:03,080
Sounds official, important.

201
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:04,800
Whereas go to is more casual.

202
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,570
Let's all go to the team huddle at 10.

203
00:08:06,790 --> 00:08:07,070
Right.

204
00:08:07,510 --> 00:08:09,630
Signals the context, the expected tone.

205
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:11,490
Same idea for participating.

206
00:08:11,750 --> 00:08:14,340
Participate in a meeting versus take part in a meeting.

207
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:14,600
Yep.

208
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:18,490
Participate feels a bit more formal, often implying active contribution.

209
00:08:18,750 --> 00:08:21,000
Did everyone participate fully in the discussion?

210
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:24,550
And take part is a bit more general, maybe just being there and involved.

211
00:08:24,730 --> 00:08:26,310
Did everyone take part in the planning session?

212
00:08:26,430 --> 00:08:26,850
Kind of.

213
00:08:26,970 --> 00:08:28,640
It's subtle, but the choice matters.

214
00:08:29,060 --> 00:08:30,500
And for organizing a meeting.

215
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:32,260
Hold a meeting or have a meeting.

216
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:34,350
Again, formal versus less formal.

217
00:08:34,610 --> 00:08:36,070
They held an emergency meeting.

218
00:08:36,190 --> 00:08:37,790
Sounds serious official.

219
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:40,240
Compared to, we had a quick meeting this morning.

220
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:42,750
More every day, less structured maybe.

221
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:43,490
Exactly.

222
00:08:43,530 --> 00:08:44,710
And leading it.

223
00:08:44,890 --> 00:08:50,030
Ah, chair a meeting sounds very formal, like specific duties.

224
00:08:50,190 --> 00:08:50,590
It does.

225
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:55,100
It implies being the chairperson, guiding the agenda, managing time formally.

226
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:58,190
Professor Evans will chair the academic committee meeting.

227
00:08:58,250 --> 00:09:00,690
Versus run or lead a meeting.

228
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:01,650
Much less formal.

229
00:09:02,130 --> 00:09:06,460
Still in charge, facilitating, but maybe without the strict procedural feel.

230
00:09:07,190 --> 00:09:09,130
Sarah will run the project update meeting.

231
00:09:09,370 --> 00:09:09,650
Got it.

232
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:10,530
And reschedule it.

233
00:09:10,610 --> 00:09:12,760
Postpone a meeting is the formal way.

234
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:15,360
Usually written, maybe for important events.

235
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:17,990
We must postpone the client presentation until next week.

236
00:09:18,020 --> 00:09:20,140
And put off a meeting is the casual version.

237
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:22,030
Sorry, have to put off our coffee chat.

238
00:09:22,170 --> 00:09:22,550
Exactly.

239
00:09:22,590 --> 00:09:23,470
Just more informal.

240
00:09:23,810 --> 00:09:26,640
Knowing these pairs lets you match your language to the situation.

241
00:09:26,780 --> 00:09:27,960
Definitely projects awareness.

242
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:35,100
Okay, so beyond the verbs, what about describing the meetings themselves, the different types and how they're structured?

243
00:09:35,150 --> 00:09:35,470
Right.

244
00:09:35,910 --> 00:09:38,130
Describing meetings and their structure.

245
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:40,340
Useful vocabulary here too.

246
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:42,900
Sometimes you just need a brief meeting.

247
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:44,400
Short and sweet limited time.

248
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:45,850
Let's have a brief meeting before the call.

249
00:09:46,010 --> 00:09:46,450
Exactly.

250
00:09:46,510 --> 00:09:47,430
Sets expectations.

251
00:09:47,770 --> 00:09:49,830
Then there are times for an urgent meeting.

252
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:50,900
Crisis mode.

253
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:52,700
Problem needs immediate attention.

254
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,710
They called an urgent meeting about the system outage.

255
00:09:55,850 --> 00:09:56,650
Precisely.

256
00:09:56,690 --> 00:10:02,200
High priority signals seriousness and for keeping track of actions.

257
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:05,570
A follow-up meeting to check progress from a previous one?

258
00:10:05,610 --> 00:10:06,090
Yeah.

259
00:10:06,710 --> 00:10:09,650
Let's schedule a follow-up meeting next week to review the action items.

260
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:11,480
Crucial for accountability.

261
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:12,100
Makes sense.

262
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:13,240
And starting something new.

263
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:14,980
That's often a kickoff meeting.

264
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:18,330
The official start of a project usually involves the whole team.

265
00:10:18,490 --> 00:10:18,690
Right.

266
00:10:18,830 --> 00:10:20,170
Setting goals, defining scope.

267
00:10:20,410 --> 00:10:22,980
The kickoff meeting for Project Phoenix is on Tuesday.

268
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:24,380
Gets everyone aligned.

269
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:29,230
Now, for structure within a formal meeting, the agenda is key.

270
00:10:29,250 --> 00:10:31,840
The list of topics to cover, usually sent out beforehand.

271
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:34,040
Did you get the agenda for tomorrow's board meeting?

272
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:34,400
Yep.

273
00:10:34,890 --> 00:10:35,810
Keeps things on track.

274
00:10:36,230 --> 00:10:37,990
And the specific points on that list.

275
00:10:38,090 --> 00:10:38,970
Items on the agenda.

276
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:39,530
Simple enough.

277
00:10:40,210 --> 00:10:40,320
Yeah.

278
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:42,300
Okay, let's move to the next item on the agenda.

279
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:44,920
Guides the flow and capturing what happened.

280
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:45,960
Ah, to take the minutes.

281
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:48,320
Recording the official decisions and actions.

282
00:10:48,340 --> 00:10:48,900
Exactly.

283
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:51,190
And the record itself is called The Minutes.

284
00:10:51,530 --> 00:10:53,810
Can someone volunteer to take the minutes today?

285
00:10:53,850 --> 00:10:55,890
Super important for having a record.

286
00:10:56,050 --> 00:10:56,490
Absolutely.

287
00:10:56,750 --> 00:10:59,360
Without minutes, things can get lost or debated later.

288
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:00,300
Yeah.

289
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:01,460
Okay, so that covers the mechanics.

290
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,240
What about the actual discussion?

291
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:06,230
Expressing yourself effectively.

292
00:11:06,290 --> 00:11:27,270
critical part let's talk about expressing opinions effectively moving beyond just I think yes how to ask for opinions first instead of just what do you think right try something more inviting like what are your thoughts on for example what are your thoughts on this new proposal it encourages a more detailed answer okay shows you value their perspective what else

293
00:11:27,450 --> 00:11:32,640
How do you feel about, this often taps into more personal or emotional responses, which can be useful.

294
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:35,100
How do you feel about the potential office move?

295
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:38,580
Gets buy-in, understands morale, good one.

296
00:11:39,060 --> 00:11:47,230
And for broader topics, do you have any views on, very open-ended, do you have any views on improving inter-department communication?

297
00:11:48,090 --> 00:11:48,450
Nice.

298
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:52,820
These feel more professional, more engaging, now giving opinions.

299
00:11:52,970 --> 00:11:56,840
Okay, instead of just, I think, the way I see it, it sounds more considered.

300
00:11:57,100 --> 00:12:00,250
The way I see it, we should prioritize the customer feedback.

301
00:12:00,550 --> 00:12:01,850
Implies you've analyzed it.

302
00:12:02,010 --> 00:12:02,670
I like that.

303
00:12:02,790 --> 00:12:07,490
Or, as far as I'm concerned, this one's quite direct, states your personal conviction clearly.

304
00:12:07,530 --> 00:12:11,040
As far as I'm concerned, Sarah is the right choice for the team lead.

305
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:11,540
Okay.

306
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:13,160
Strong personal view.

307
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:14,620
And more formal.

308
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:15,540
In my view.

309
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:18,270
Clear, professional, objective sounding.

310
00:12:18,530 --> 00:12:21,130
In my view, the data supports launching in Q3.

311
00:12:21,510 --> 00:12:22,210
Good options.

312
00:12:22,730 --> 00:12:24,170
What if you feel really strongly?

313
00:12:24,410 --> 00:12:26,200
How do you express that without just shouting?

314
00:12:26,390 --> 00:12:27,280
Haha, right.

315
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:34,490
For strong opinions, you can use phrases like, I'm absolutely convinced that, high degree of certainty, I'm absolutely convinced this new approach will work.

316
00:12:34,510 --> 00:12:35,850
Conveys strong belief.

317
00:12:35,870 --> 00:12:37,290
Or, I strongly believe that.

318
00:12:37,670 --> 00:12:39,860
Similar, emphasizes deep conviction.

319
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:43,240
I strongly believe we need to invest more in R&D.

320
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:44,810
Okay, powerful.

321
00:12:45,210 --> 00:12:45,790
And one more.

322
00:12:46,310 --> 00:12:48,050
I have no doubt that.

323
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:50,600
Remember the silent B in doubt.

324
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:51,840
Pronounce doubt.

325
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:54,200
I have no doubt that the team can meet this challenge.

326
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:56,370
Expresses complete confidence.

327
00:12:56,670 --> 00:12:57,070
Got it.

328
00:12:57,510 --> 00:12:57,830
No B.

329
00:12:58,170 --> 00:13:00,360
These are great for adding impact when needed.

330
00:13:00,390 --> 00:13:02,080
Use them wisely, though.

331
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:06,430
Too much conviction on everything can seem, well, a bit much.

332
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:06,960
True.

333
00:13:07,580 --> 00:13:08,860
OK, so you've expressed your view.

334
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:12,950
What about reacting to others, agreeing and disagreeing?

335
00:13:13,170 --> 00:13:14,870
That's the art of agreement and disagreement.

336
00:13:14,990 --> 00:13:15,250
Yes.

337
00:13:15,510 --> 00:13:17,570
And this involves a lot of social intelligence.

338
00:13:17,650 --> 00:13:20,320
Let's start with agreeing instead of just, yes, try.

339
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:21,060
Absolutely.

340
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:22,700
It's quick, emphatic.

341
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:23,600
Shows enthusiasm.

342
00:13:23,820 --> 00:13:24,660
Or that makes sense.

343
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:27,110
Shows you understand the logic behind their point.

344
00:13:27,230 --> 00:13:27,570
Good one.

345
00:13:27,730 --> 00:13:28,770
Validates their reasoning.

346
00:13:28,850 --> 00:13:30,370
Maybe I'd go along with that.

347
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:33,620
Suggest you're willing to support the idea, showing flexibility.

348
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:34,250
Okay.

349
00:13:34,260 --> 00:13:36,270
What about really strong agreement?

350
00:13:36,290 --> 00:13:39,270
For strongly agreeing, you can say, I couldn't agree more.

351
00:13:39,690 --> 00:13:40,950
Shows complete alignment.

352
00:13:41,070 --> 00:13:41,490
Powerful.

353
00:13:41,630 --> 00:13:42,770
Or you're absolutely right.

354
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:47,020
Stress absolutely for emphasis really validates them.

355
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:47,420
Nice.

356
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:49,940
And that feeling when someone says exactly what you were thinking.

357
00:13:51,250 --> 00:13:51,590
Ah, yes.

358
00:13:51,730 --> 00:13:53,230
That's exactly what I wanted to say.

359
00:13:53,570 --> 00:13:54,770
Again, stress exactly.

360
00:13:55,170 --> 00:13:56,650
Shows perfect alignment of thought.

361
00:13:57,410 --> 00:13:57,790
Love that one.

362
00:13:58,150 --> 00:13:58,410
Okay.

363
00:13:58,450 --> 00:13:59,290
Now the tricky part.

364
00:14:00,620 --> 00:14:21,690
disagreement especially partial agreement when you agree with some but not all this is where diplomacy shines try i agree with you up to a point but acknowledges some validity then introduces your reservation like i agree with you up to a point about the deadline but is it realistic without more resources

365
00:14:21,750 --> 00:14:22,580
Perfect example.

366
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:27,520
Or, I see where you're coming from, but shows empathy, understanding their reasoning first.

367
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:29,170
I see where you're coming from with that strategy.

368
00:14:29,270 --> 00:14:29,550
Yeah.

369
00:14:29,570 --> 00:14:31,390
But have we considered the potential risks?

370
00:14:31,810 --> 00:14:33,250
Builds a bridge before disagreeing.

371
00:14:33,390 --> 00:14:33,840
Exactly.

372
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:36,460
Softens the disagreement, makes it collaborative.

373
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:39,350
Now, for outright disagreeing, politeness is key.

374
00:14:39,570 --> 00:14:39,770
Yeah.

375
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:41,870
Often preface with, I'm afraid.

376
00:14:41,850 --> 00:14:43,770
Like, I'm afraid I can't go along with that.

377
00:14:43,910 --> 00:14:44,740
Polite but firm.

378
00:14:44,910 --> 00:14:46,240
Yes, or gentler.

379
00:14:46,340 --> 00:14:47,400
I'm not so sure about that.

380
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:50,300
Expresses doubt without direct contradiction.

381
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:52,470
Invites discussion rather than conflict.

382
00:14:52,490 --> 00:14:54,290
Right, or slightly more formal.

383
00:14:54,350 --> 00:14:55,450
I'm not sure I would agree with that.

384
00:14:55,810 --> 00:14:56,570
Again, softens it.

385
00:14:56,930 --> 00:15:00,340
Okay, but what if you need to strongly disagree?

386
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:03,440
Something is just wrong or unacceptable.

387
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:08,520
For strongly disagreeing, the language gets more direct, but tone is crucial.

388
00:15:08,850 --> 00:15:10,510
You can't be serious.

389
00:15:11,110 --> 00:15:13,880
Use with care expresses shock or disbelief.

390
00:15:14,260 --> 00:15:15,680
Yeah, context is everything there.

391
00:15:15,780 --> 00:15:17,880
Or I'm sorry, but that's totally unacceptable.

392
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:20,430
Stress totally marks a clear boundary.

393
00:15:20,750 --> 00:15:21,650
Yeah, very clear.

394
00:15:21,670 --> 00:15:22,570
And perhaps the strongest.

395
00:15:22,950 --> 00:15:25,240
I'm sorry, but there's no way I can accept that.

396
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:26,780
Stress, no way.

397
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:29,420
Leave zero room for negotiation on that point.

398
00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:34,370
These are for serious situations, then, not for everyday disagreements about coffee preferences.

399
00:15:34,410 --> 00:15:35,090
Definitely not.

400
00:15:35,370 --> 00:15:36,830
Reserve them for when it truly matters.

401
00:15:36,870 --> 00:15:39,140
Use them sparingly for maximum impact.

402
00:15:39,260 --> 00:15:39,460
Right.

403
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:40,080
Okay.

404
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:40,580
Makes sense.

405
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:42,600
Now, meetings can get messy.

406
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:43,680
People talk over each other.

407
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:46,060
Let's talk about handling interruptions gracefully.

408
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:46,900
Ah, yes.

409
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:47,900
A common challenge.

410
00:15:48,490 --> 00:15:51,030
First, phrases for interrupting politely when you need to.

411
00:15:51,150 --> 00:15:52,290
Instead of just jumping in.

412
00:15:52,270 --> 00:15:52,590
Right.

413
00:15:53,310 --> 00:16:00,020
Before you go on, could you, good for asking a quick clarifying question, before you go on, could you explain that acronym?

414
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:01,110
Polite stop.

415
00:16:01,260 --> 00:16:01,530
Okay.

416
00:16:01,930 --> 00:16:03,530
Or can I just add something here?

417
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:06,110
If you have a relevant quick point.

418
00:16:06,350 --> 00:16:07,700
Signals a brief contribution.

419
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:09,860
You could also say, can I just stop you there?

420
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:13,100
A bit more direct, but still okay if done politely.

421
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:14,760
And always good.

422
00:16:15,210 --> 00:16:17,370
Sorry for interrupting, but shows awareness.

423
00:16:17,570 --> 00:16:17,830
Okay.

424
00:16:17,970 --> 00:16:19,270
Those are useful for interjecting.

425
00:16:19,290 --> 00:16:21,210
What about when you get interrupted?

426
00:16:21,650 --> 00:16:23,680
Handling interruptions directed at you.

427
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:24,440
You have options.

428
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:25,620
You can politely defer.

429
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:28,130
I'll answer that question later if you don't mind.

430
00:16:28,150 --> 00:16:28,250
Yeah.

431
00:16:29,130 --> 00:16:30,550
Buys you time to finish your thought.

432
00:16:30,870 --> 00:16:32,430
Acknowledges but postpones.

433
00:16:32,450 --> 00:16:32,840
Yeah.

434
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:33,050
Good.

435
00:16:33,520 --> 00:16:34,400
Or just accept it.

436
00:16:34,420 --> 00:16:34,700
Simple.

437
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:35,540
Yes, go ahead.

438
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:37,720
If you're happy to yield the floor.

439
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:38,350
Okay.

440
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:40,150
But if you need to finish.

441
00:16:40,210 --> 00:16:41,170
Be firm but polite.

442
00:16:41,930 --> 00:16:42,740
Let me finish, please.

443
00:16:42,910 --> 00:16:43,510
Clear signal.

444
00:16:43,680 --> 00:16:44,060
Direct.

445
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:46,920
Or if you lost your train of thought, I'm almost finished my point.

446
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:47,500
Where was I?

447
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:51,240
Human, relatable, signals intent to continue.

448
00:16:51,500 --> 00:16:52,060
I like that one.

449
00:16:52,180 --> 00:16:54,250
And after the interruption, how do you get back on track?

450
00:16:54,470 --> 00:16:55,290
Returning to your point.

451
00:16:55,570 --> 00:16:56,070
Smoothly.

452
00:16:56,490 --> 00:16:59,830
Use phrases like, as I was saying, or as I was explaining.

453
00:16:59,970 --> 00:17:00,890
Simple transition back.

454
00:17:00,970 --> 00:17:01,790
Okay, thanks for that point.

455
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:04,400
As I was saying, the key issue is...

456
00:17:04,380 --> 00:17:05,020
Exactly.

457
00:17:05,060 --> 00:17:08,700
It helps maintain control and flow, even with interruptions.

458
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:10,980
Like verbal traffic management.

459
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:12,710
Okay, that covers meanings pretty thoroughly.

460
00:17:13,330 --> 00:17:15,090
Now let's shift to another common area.

461
00:17:15,790 --> 00:17:16,810
Problems and decisions.

462
00:17:17,590 --> 00:17:18,550
Addressing challenges.

463
00:17:19,270 --> 00:17:21,300
Vocabulary for problems and decisions.

464
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:22,300
Crucial area.

465
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:26,320
How you talk about problems shapes how they're perceived and tackled.

466
00:17:26,820 --> 00:17:28,380
Let's start with describing problems.

467
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:30,160
Nuance terminology.

468
00:17:30,330 --> 00:17:32,110
Beyond just, we have a problem.

469
00:17:32,210 --> 00:17:32,430
Right.

470
00:17:32,470 --> 00:17:37,860
You can use have problems with, trouble, difficulties, or issues, often followed by a noun.

471
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:40,960
They're similar, but offer slight variations.

472
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:46,470
So we're having problems with the supplier, having trouble with the supplier, difficulties, issues, all work.

473
00:17:46,750 --> 00:17:47,190
Pretty much.

474
00:17:47,470 --> 00:17:50,690
Trouble is often singular, like having trouble with the new software.

475
00:17:51,130 --> 00:17:54,700
Issues can sometimes sound a bit softer or more corporate, maybe.

476
00:17:54,940 --> 00:17:55,600
Okay.

477
00:17:55,620 --> 00:17:57,740
And describing things is just plain hard.

478
00:17:57,750 --> 00:18:01,560
Yeah, it's very difficult to do or it's quite hard to do or about people.

479
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:05,330
We find it difficult to or he finds it hard to.

480
00:18:05,370 --> 00:18:10,680
Like it's difficult to predict the market or we find it hard to recruit experienced engineers right now.

481
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:11,570
Exactly.

482
00:18:11,650 --> 00:18:12,910
Clearly states the challenge.

483
00:18:13,430 --> 00:18:17,680
Now, adding adjectives really helps define the severity or nature of the problem.

484
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:18,720
Like minor problem.

485
00:18:19,140 --> 00:18:19,800
Not a big deal.

486
00:18:19,840 --> 00:18:23,260
Yeah, small, manageable, just a minor issue with the formatting.

487
00:18:23,610 --> 00:18:24,270
Easily fixed.

488
00:18:24,650 --> 00:18:25,390
Then the opposite.

489
00:18:25,990 --> 00:18:26,830
Major problem.

490
00:18:27,090 --> 00:18:28,670
Very serious, big consequences.

491
00:18:29,130 --> 00:18:31,260
Losing that client would be a major problem.

492
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:31,720
Right.

493
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:33,720
Signals high importance.

494
00:18:33,740 --> 00:18:35,720
Then there's immediate problem.

495
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:36,760
Use attention right now.

496
00:18:37,420 --> 00:18:37,770
Urgent.

497
00:18:38,430 --> 00:18:40,670
Cash flow is an immediate problem we need to solve this week.

498
00:18:40,890 --> 00:18:41,470
Exactly.

499
00:18:41,890 --> 00:18:42,910
Highlights urgency.

500
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:44,920
And if a problem keeps happening.

501
00:18:45,050 --> 00:18:45,970
A persistent problem.

502
00:18:46,730 --> 00:18:47,970
Recurring, maybe systemic.

503
00:18:48,670 --> 00:18:51,050
We have persistent problems with the server crashing.

504
00:18:51,070 --> 00:18:51,360
Yes.

505
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:54,220
Suggests it's ongoing and needs more than a quick fix.

506
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:56,280
These adjectives add vital context.

507
00:18:56,440 --> 00:18:57,830
Okay, so you've described the problem.

508
00:18:58,090 --> 00:18:59,330
Now you need to solve it.

509
00:18:59,350 --> 00:19:00,210
Solving problems.

510
00:19:00,870 --> 00:19:01,930
Action-oriented verbs.

511
00:19:02,370 --> 00:19:02,630
Right.

512
00:19:02,770 --> 00:19:04,410
Verbs that show you're doing something about it.

513
00:19:04,680 --> 00:19:07,940
To tackle a problem sounds proactive, determined.

514
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:10,610
We need to tackle the issue of low morale head on.

515
00:19:10,790 --> 00:19:11,490
Good example.

516
00:19:12,050 --> 00:19:15,990
Similar, but maybe slightly more systematic, is to address a problem.

517
00:19:16,330 --> 00:19:19,060
Like dealing with it directly, maybe investigating.

518
00:19:19,820 --> 00:19:22,240
We need to address the points raised in the customer survey.

519
00:19:22,380 --> 00:19:22,680
Yeah.

520
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:29,930
And to deal with a problem implies managing or handling it effectively often highlights competence.

521
00:19:30,170 --> 00:19:32,070
She's great at dealing with unexpected crises.

522
00:19:32,290 --> 00:19:32,870
Exactly.

523
00:19:32,930 --> 00:19:35,710
These verbs show you're moving towards solutions.

524
00:19:35,690 --> 00:19:36,260
Okay.

525
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:37,640
Now, you mentioned idioms earlier.

526
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:40,650
Let's dive into idioms for problems and difficulties.

527
00:19:40,690 --> 00:19:41,790
They add color, right?

528
00:19:41,850 --> 00:19:42,410
They really do.

529
00:19:42,590 --> 00:19:45,160
Make your English sound more natural and vivid.

530
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:47,740
First up, come to a head.

531
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:52,250
Meaning a situation reaches a crisis point, needs immediate action.

532
00:19:52,430 --> 00:19:53,060
Precisely.

533
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:57,120
Things were tense for weeks, but they finally came to a head during the budget meeting.

534
00:19:57,240 --> 00:19:58,380
Okay, a boiling point.

535
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:00,200
What about in dire straits?

536
00:20:00,530 --> 00:20:05,330
Means in a really, really difficult situation, often financially very serious.

537
00:20:05,530 --> 00:20:08,150
The company was in dire straits after the product recall.

538
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:09,380
Sounds bad.

539
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:10,040
It does.

540
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:12,160
Then a common one for new things.

541
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:13,640
Teething problems.

542
00:20:13,900 --> 00:20:16,360
Ah, those initial glitches when you start something new.

543
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:17,570
Usually temporary.

544
00:20:17,610 --> 00:20:18,310
Exactly.

545
00:20:18,690 --> 00:20:22,200
We had a few teething problems with the new website launch, but it's running smoothly now.

546
00:20:22,660 --> 00:20:23,640
Reassuring term.

547
00:20:23,660 --> 00:20:27,090
Yeah, acknowledges issues but frames them as normal and solvable.

548
00:20:27,570 --> 00:20:28,590
And a hopeful one.

549
00:20:28,610 --> 00:20:29,840
See light at the end of the tunnel.

550
00:20:30,620 --> 00:20:32,480
Signs of improvement after a tough time.

551
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:38,170
Sales were down for ages, but with a new campaign, we're finally seeing light at the end of the tunnel.

552
00:20:38,610 --> 00:20:39,550
Morale booster.

553
00:20:39,650 --> 00:20:40,250
Definitely.

554
00:20:40,290 --> 00:20:43,300
These idioms really enrich your descriptions.

555
00:20:43,420 --> 00:20:43,660
Okay.

556
00:20:43,820 --> 00:20:50,890
From problems to decisions, navigating decisions, key combinations, using adjectives with decision.

557
00:20:50,970 --> 00:20:51,210
Right.

558
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:53,250
A key decision is a really important one.

559
00:20:53,350 --> 00:20:54,010
High stakes.

560
00:20:54,270 --> 00:20:57,300
Choosing the next CEO is a key decision for the board.

561
00:20:57,540 --> 00:20:58,080
Exactly.

562
00:20:58,120 --> 00:21:01,500
Then a tough decision, difficult choice, hard trade-offs.

563
00:21:01,780 --> 00:21:03,600
Letting people go is always a tough decision.

564
00:21:04,500 --> 00:21:07,770
And unfortunately, sometimes there's a poor decision, just a bad call.

565
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:11,370
Investing in that failed technology was a poor decision in hindsight.

566
00:21:11,420 --> 00:21:11,670
Right.

567
00:21:12,070 --> 00:21:14,670
Labels the outcome and the act of deciding.

568
00:21:14,790 --> 00:21:17,520
Come to a decision, especially after thinking it over.

569
00:21:17,630 --> 00:21:17,900
Yeah.

570
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:19,360
Emphasizes the process.

571
00:21:19,640 --> 00:21:22,660
Have they come to a decision about the office location yet?

572
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:23,240
Okay.

573
00:21:23,610 --> 00:21:25,590
And the person who decides.

574
00:21:25,990 --> 00:21:27,310
The key decision maker.

575
00:21:27,850 --> 00:21:29,910
The one with the authority for those big calls.

576
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:32,120
You need to talk to the CFO.

577
00:21:32,600 --> 00:21:34,720
She's the key decision maker on budgets.

578
00:21:34,980 --> 00:21:36,280
Clarifies who holds the power.

579
00:21:36,830 --> 00:21:37,030
Good.

580
00:21:37,670 --> 00:21:39,250
What about the person making the decision?

581
00:21:39,810 --> 00:21:40,510
Their style.

582
00:21:41,150 --> 00:21:43,110
That's decisive communication.

583
00:21:43,170 --> 00:21:46,000
Adjectives and phrases for decision making.

584
00:21:46,420 --> 00:21:50,000
Someone who decides quickly and effectively is decisive.

585
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:51,280
The good leadership trait.

586
00:21:52,010 --> 00:21:53,750
She's very decisive under pressure.

587
00:21:53,910 --> 00:21:54,210
Yes.

588
00:21:54,690 --> 00:21:56,260
The opposite is indecisive.

589
00:21:56,820 --> 00:21:57,760
Struggles to choose.

590
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,400
His indecisive nature slows down the whole team.

591
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:01,610
Can be frustrating.

592
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:02,050
Very.

593
00:22:02,250 --> 00:22:04,090
Now, changing your mind or having doubts.

594
00:22:04,910 --> 00:22:05,730
To have second thoughts.

595
00:22:06,230 --> 00:22:07,430
Doubting a choice you already made.

596
00:22:07,650 --> 00:22:08,070
Exactly.

597
00:22:08,170 --> 00:22:13,080
I accepted the offer, but now I'm having second thoughts about the long commute uncertainty creeps in.

598
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:14,660
And just simply altering your choice.

599
00:22:14,820 --> 00:22:15,660
To change your mind.

600
00:22:15,850 --> 00:22:16,470
Straightforward.

601
00:22:16,830 --> 00:22:18,930
He was going to join the project, but he changed his mind.

602
00:22:18,990 --> 00:22:19,270
Okay.

603
00:22:19,710 --> 00:22:21,830
And that useful phrase for delegating a choice.

604
00:22:21,860 --> 00:22:26,290
It's up to you, meaning you make the call, coffee or tea, it's up to you.

605
00:22:26,810 --> 00:22:29,600
Simple, but it can apply to others too, right?

606
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:30,480
Absolutely.

607
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:31,140
It's up to her.

608
00:22:31,220 --> 00:22:31,980
It's up to the committee.

609
00:22:32,420 --> 00:22:34,360
Clarifies who has the responsibility.

610
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:36,170
We've presented the options.

611
00:22:36,390 --> 00:22:37,730
Now it's up to the client.

612
00:22:37,930 --> 00:22:39,870
Very useful for avoiding ambiguity.

613
00:22:40,310 --> 00:22:42,910
Okay, that's a solid look at problems and decisions.

614
00:22:43,360 --> 00:22:43,840
What's next?

615
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:45,720
Let's get into something always relevant.

616
00:22:46,340 --> 00:22:48,060
Money, experience, performance.

617
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:49,340
Your professional toolkit.

618
00:22:49,880 --> 00:22:51,620
Salary, experience, and performance.

619
00:22:51,900 --> 00:22:54,750
Mastering this language is vital for career navigation.

620
00:22:55,030 --> 00:22:55,370
Agreed.

621
00:22:55,770 --> 00:22:57,610
Let's start with the money talk.

622
00:22:57,630 --> 00:22:58,950
Understanding compensation.

623
00:22:59,390 --> 00:23:00,540
The language of pay.

624
00:23:01,340 --> 00:23:02,740
Gross versus net.

625
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:03,020
Right.

626
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:07,950
Gross salary or gross pay is the big number, the total before anything is taken out.

627
00:23:08,010 --> 00:23:09,830
Before tax, insurance, all that stuff.

628
00:23:09,950 --> 00:23:10,550
Exactly.

629
00:23:10,610 --> 00:23:12,700
Those things taken out are called deductions.

630
00:23:13,180 --> 00:23:17,900
Tax, health insurance premiums, perhaps pension contributions for retirement savings.

631
00:23:17,920 --> 00:23:19,590
So the gross isn't what you actually get.

632
00:23:19,730 --> 00:23:20,050
Nope.

633
00:23:20,070 --> 00:23:23,840
What you actually get in your bank account is the net salary or net pay.

634
00:23:23,860 --> 00:23:24,980
That's after all deductions.

635
00:23:25,020 --> 00:23:26,060
In the informal term?

636
00:23:26,210 --> 00:23:27,010
Take-home pay.

637
00:23:27,330 --> 00:23:27,930
Very common.

638
00:23:28,150 --> 00:23:30,930
My take-home pay is around X amount per month.

639
00:23:30,960 --> 00:23:31,460
Good to know.

640
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:35,540
Though maybe be careful discussing specific numbers culturally.

641
00:23:35,900 --> 00:23:36,400
Absolutely.

642
00:23:36,700 --> 00:23:37,610
Very important point.

643
00:23:37,970 --> 00:23:40,110
It's often considered private, so discretion is key.

644
00:23:40,390 --> 00:23:40,670
Okay.

645
00:23:40,990 --> 00:23:42,070
What about getting more money?

646
00:23:42,890 --> 00:23:44,540
Career progression and benefits.

647
00:23:44,590 --> 00:23:45,710
Everyone's favorite topic.

648
00:23:46,230 --> 00:23:51,450
An increase in salary is a pay rise or more commonly in the U.S., a pay raise.

649
00:23:51,550 --> 00:23:55,720
And you often discuss this during a performance appraisal or performance review.

650
00:23:55,940 --> 00:23:56,440
Exactly.

651
00:23:56,680 --> 00:24:01,720
That formal meeting with your boss, usually yearly, to discuss how you've done, your goals, etc.

652
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:05,390
I'm preparing my case for a pay rise for my performance review.

653
00:24:05,550 --> 00:24:07,610
Might involve a self-evaluation first.

654
00:24:07,650 --> 00:24:07,870
Yeah.

655
00:24:07,890 --> 00:24:09,110
Assessing your own performance.

656
00:24:09,370 --> 00:24:10,470
Often, yes.

657
00:24:10,450 --> 00:24:12,180
Good chance to highlight your achievements.

658
00:24:12,460 --> 00:24:14,220
Now working extra hours.

659
00:24:14,260 --> 00:24:15,460
That's overtime.

660
00:24:15,480 --> 00:24:17,280
Hours beyond your standard contract.

661
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:17,500
Right.

662
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:18,740
And compensation can vary.

663
00:24:18,760 --> 00:24:21,520
It might be extra pay or it might be time off in lieu.

664
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:22,240
Toil.

665
00:24:22,530 --> 00:24:22,940
Toil.

666
00:24:23,650 --> 00:24:26,570
Meaning you take the time off later instead of getting paid extra.

667
00:24:26,630 --> 00:24:27,130
Precisely.

668
00:24:27,390 --> 00:24:29,870
Work five extra hours, get five hours off later.

669
00:24:30,170 --> 00:24:31,510
Good to know how your company handles it.

670
00:24:31,710 --> 00:24:32,110
Definitely.

671
00:24:32,590 --> 00:24:32,970
Okay.

672
00:24:32,990 --> 00:24:34,150
How do we describe pay levels?

673
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:36,080
Describing pay and added value.

674
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:38,940
If you feel you're not paid enough for your work, you're underpaid.

675
00:24:39,180 --> 00:24:40,460
Common complaint, maybe.

676
00:24:40,490 --> 00:24:42,540
He feels overworked and underpaid.

677
00:24:43,380 --> 00:24:44,480
Yeah, hear that a lot.

678
00:24:45,180 --> 00:24:46,040
And the opposite.

679
00:24:46,280 --> 00:24:48,170
A job that pays well is well paid.

680
00:24:48,830 --> 00:24:50,970
And if someone thinks the pay is too high for the work.

681
00:24:51,390 --> 00:24:52,290
Then they're overpaid.

682
00:24:52,570 --> 00:24:53,780
Like those footballer examples.

683
00:24:53,820 --> 00:24:54,060
Right.

684
00:24:54,240 --> 00:24:55,520
These terms are evaluative.

685
00:24:56,180 --> 00:24:58,260
Now, beyond salary, there's commission.

686
00:24:58,330 --> 00:24:59,870
Extra pay based on sales.

687
00:25:00,310 --> 00:25:01,390
Common in sales rules.

688
00:25:01,590 --> 00:25:02,050
Exactly.

689
00:25:02,250 --> 00:25:03,360
A percentage of what you sell.

690
00:25:03,700 --> 00:25:06,760
She earns a good base salary plus 10% commission.

691
00:25:07,240 --> 00:25:08,160
Then there's a bonus.

692
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:09,220
Extra payment.

693
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:10,570
Often tied to performance targets.

694
00:25:10,950 --> 00:25:12,090
Individual or company.

695
00:25:12,390 --> 00:25:13,090
Usually, yes.

696
00:25:13,750 --> 00:25:16,730
We might get a Christmas bonus if the company hits its targets.

697
00:25:16,750 --> 00:25:18,180
And non-cash benefits.

698
00:25:18,480 --> 00:25:19,500
Those are perks.

699
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:22,820
Like gym memberships, free snacks, good health insurance.

700
00:25:23,180 --> 00:25:25,510
Things that make the job attractive beyond the salary.

701
00:25:25,690 --> 00:25:26,290
Exactly.

702
00:25:26,370 --> 00:25:28,150
Part of the total compensation picture.

703
00:25:28,130 --> 00:25:29,110
Okay, very clear.

704
00:25:29,450 --> 00:25:30,510
Let's move to experience.

705
00:25:30,950 --> 00:25:32,640
Gaining and describing experience.

706
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:34,620
Crucial for CVs and interviews.

707
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:35,440
Absolutely.

708
00:25:35,800 --> 00:25:37,740
Practical or hands-on experience is key.

709
00:25:38,110 --> 00:25:40,390
Means you learned by doing, not just reading.

710
00:25:40,810 --> 00:25:44,030
The internship gave me valuable hands-on experience in marketing.

711
00:25:44,450 --> 00:25:46,000
Shows real-world application.

712
00:25:46,380 --> 00:25:46,640
Right.

713
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:49,240
Similar idea with first-hand experience.

714
00:25:49,700 --> 00:25:51,610
Gain directly by doing it yourself.

715
00:25:51,820 --> 00:25:58,010
He has firsthand experience managing large budgets implies direct involvement and not having enough.

716
00:25:58,170 --> 00:25:59,760
That's a lack of experience.

717
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:01,960
Simple but can be a barrier.

718
00:26:02,180 --> 00:26:04,710
Her lack of client facing experience was a concern.

719
00:26:05,090 --> 00:26:06,530
OK. And having lots of good experience.

720
00:26:06,550 --> 00:26:07,970
You have a wealth of experience.

721
00:26:08,090 --> 00:26:08,930
Sounds impressive.

722
00:26:09,060 --> 00:26:10,460
Suggests deep knowledge.

723
00:26:11,020 --> 00:26:14,180
Our new consultant brings a wealth of experience in the energy sector.

724
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:15,510
Nice phrase.

725
00:26:15,530 --> 00:26:17,270
And just generally useful experience.

726
00:26:17,290 --> 00:26:18,270
Valuable experience.

727
00:26:18,510 --> 00:26:20,390
Helpful for your skills or career.

728
00:26:20,550 --> 00:26:22,710
Working abroad was a really valuable experience.

729
00:26:22,730 --> 00:26:23,760
And you can combine them, right?

730
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:25,580
Valuable hands-on experience.

731
00:26:25,780 --> 00:26:26,240
Good point.

732
00:26:26,480 --> 00:26:28,240
Emphasizes the quality and nature.

733
00:26:29,240 --> 00:26:31,720
Okay, finally, showing consistent performance.

734
00:26:32,570 --> 00:26:34,610
Showcasing past performance.

735
00:26:34,630 --> 00:26:35,410
The track record.

736
00:26:35,570 --> 00:26:35,750
Yes.

737
00:26:36,110 --> 00:26:38,490
Track record is about your history of performance.

738
00:26:38,510 --> 00:26:40,010
How well you've done things over time.

739
00:26:40,410 --> 00:26:42,100
Not just a one-off success.

740
00:26:42,080 --> 00:26:46,740
So a good track record means consistently positive results, reliable.

741
00:26:46,960 --> 00:26:47,720
Exactly.

742
00:26:48,160 --> 00:26:52,490
The agency has a good track record of delivering creative campaigns, builds trust.

743
00:26:52,850 --> 00:26:54,790
Even better is an impressive track record.

744
00:26:54,950 --> 00:26:56,920
Really outstanding, noteworthy results.

745
00:26:57,600 --> 00:26:58,380
Makes you stand out.

746
00:26:58,580 --> 00:26:58,880
Yes.

747
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:02,590
She has an impressive track record of turning around failing projects.

748
00:27:03,030 --> 00:27:03,550
High praise.

749
00:27:03,910 --> 00:27:04,570
And the best kind.

750
00:27:04,590 --> 00:27:11,360
A proven track record means you've shown repeatedly with evidence that you're very good at something specific.

751
00:27:11,540 --> 00:27:15,190
We chose them because they have a proven track record in cybersecurity.

752
00:27:16,050 --> 00:27:18,500
Gold standard implies reliability.

753
00:27:18,720 --> 00:27:19,240
It really does.

754
00:27:19,400 --> 00:27:20,450
Hugely impactful phrase.

755
00:27:20,490 --> 00:27:21,910
Of course, the opposite exists.

756
00:27:22,250 --> 00:27:23,300
A poor track record.

757
00:27:23,740 --> 00:27:25,900
Consistent negative performance or issues.

758
00:27:25,930 --> 00:27:27,100
Unfortunately, yes.

759
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:32,620
The previous supplier had a poor track record on delivery times, a clear warning sign.

760
00:27:33,110 --> 00:27:37,050
Understanding track records is key for reputation and future opportunities.

761
00:27:37,310 --> 00:27:37,790
Definitely.

762
00:27:37,990 --> 00:27:38,210
Wow.

763
00:27:38,390 --> 00:27:43,320
OK, we've really covered a huge amount there from those core team verbs right through to discussing track records.

764
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:46,310
That was quite the journey through practical business English.

765
00:27:46,450 --> 00:27:47,270
It really was.

766
00:27:47,330 --> 00:27:50,400
It feels like we've gone beyond just, you know, definitions.

767
00:27:51,240 --> 00:27:55,330
It's about how these phrases actually work in real workplace conversations.

768
00:27:55,470 --> 00:27:56,010
Exactly.

769
00:27:56,030 --> 00:28:05,150
It's about the strategy behind the words, how choosing lead instead of run or address instead of just talk about a problem can subtly shift perception.

770
00:28:05,270 --> 00:28:07,410
Yeah, it's not just about sounding smart, is it?

771
00:28:07,430 --> 00:28:11,820
It's about being more effective, more persuasive, maybe more confident.

772
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:12,840
That's the core of it.

773
00:28:13,260 --> 00:28:15,690
These aren't just vocabulary items to memorize.

774
00:28:16,070 --> 00:28:16,810
They are tools.

775
00:28:17,530 --> 00:28:23,600
Tools for clarity, for influence, for building relationships, for navigating tricky situations.

776
00:28:23,770 --> 00:28:28,530
It really does feel like a shortcut to being better equipped for those professional interactions.

777
00:28:28,990 --> 00:28:31,340
Understanding the nuances makes such a difference.

778
00:28:31,360 --> 00:28:32,180
It absolutely does.

779
00:28:32,520 --> 00:28:41,120
It's often those subtle distinctions that mark out a really effective communicator, someone who not only knows English but knows how to use business English strategically.

780
00:28:41,620 --> 00:28:44,600
So here's something for you, our learners, to maybe think about.

781
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:45,540
Final thought.

782
00:28:45,920 --> 00:28:57,480
Of everything we've unpacked today, all those phrases for meetings, for problems, for describing experience, which one single phrase or concept really stood out to you?

783
00:28:57,960 --> 00:29:01,690
Which one will you make a conscious effort to actually use this week?

784
00:29:01,920 --> 00:29:03,140
That's a great challenge.

785
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:03,920
Think about it.

786
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:08,810
Maybe it's deciding to say, I'd like to bounce some ideas off you instead of just asking for help.

787
00:29:09,550 --> 00:29:15,320
Or maybe using, I see where you're coming from, but when you partially disagree.

788
00:29:15,620 --> 00:29:19,980
How might using that one new tool change the dynamic of your next conversation?

789
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:22,470
Or even just how you feel going into it?

790
00:29:22,710 --> 00:29:23,110
Exactly.

791
00:29:23,590 --> 00:29:25,930
Could that small change start a ripple effect?

792
00:29:26,630 --> 00:29:27,670
Boost your confidence.

793
00:29:28,130 --> 00:29:30,040
Change how others see your contribution.

794
00:29:30,100 --> 00:29:31,600
It's all about application, isn't it?

795
00:29:31,760 --> 00:29:33,970
Taking the knowledge and actually putting it into practice.

796
00:29:34,050 --> 00:29:35,530
That's where the real learning happens.

797
00:29:35,630 --> 00:29:36,290
Couldn't agree more.

798
00:29:36,590 --> 00:29:40,120
So we really encourage you to reflect on that, pick one thing, and try it out.

799
00:29:40,340 --> 00:29:43,380
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive into business English.

800
00:29:43,740 --> 00:29:45,420
We hope you found it genuinely useful.

801
00:29:45,720 --> 00:29:47,710
Yeah, I hope it helps you feel more confident out there.

802
00:29:47,990 --> 00:29:53,040
Until next time, keep learning, keep practicing, and keep communicating with clarity and impact.

803
00:30:02,370 --> 00:30:08,810
key vocabulary, useful phrases, and some tips to really sharpen your communication at work.

804
00:30:08,830 --> 00:30:09,030
Yeah.

805
00:30:09,130 --> 00:30:10,260
This is fundamental stuff.

806
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:10,880
Exactly.

807
00:30:11,280 --> 00:30:16,850
Imagine you're stepping into a big project, maybe international colleagues are involved, or it's just high stakes.

808
00:30:16,920 --> 00:30:18,010
Clarity is everything.

809
00:30:18,170 --> 00:30:18,610
Absolutely.

810
00:30:19,190 --> 00:30:20,830
Misunderstandings can be costly.

811
00:30:21,150 --> 00:30:26,690
So the material we're looking at today, it's like a toolkit for those exact situations.

812
00:30:26,730 --> 00:30:29,440
It's all about sounding more professional and more confident.

813
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:30,900
And ultimately more effective.

814
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:31,240
Right.

815
00:30:31,640 --> 00:30:36,820
Whether it's just daily team chats, running a meeting or explaining a tricky problem.

816
00:30:37,510 --> 00:30:39,750
Our mission today isn't just listing words.

817
00:30:40,170 --> 00:30:48,440
We want to unpack why they matter, how to use them strategically, make it all actionable so you can navigate work with, well, a bit more ease.

818
00:30:48,420 --> 00:30:49,260
And impact.

819
00:30:49,920 --> 00:30:51,360
Language is a tool for impact.

820
00:30:51,530 --> 00:30:52,370
Couldn't agree more.

821
00:30:52,430 --> 00:30:53,450
Okay, let's unpack this.

822
00:30:53,850 --> 00:30:54,230
Sounds good.

823
00:30:54,570 --> 00:30:55,210
Where do we start?

824
00:30:55,650 --> 00:30:57,620
Let's start with the building blocks.

825
00:30:58,180 --> 00:31:01,490
Section one, the language of teams and teamwork.

826
00:31:02,010 --> 00:31:05,380
Because, let's face it, almost everything gets done in teams these days.

827
00:31:05,430 --> 00:31:06,050
It really does.

828
00:31:06,710 --> 00:31:10,110
Collaboration is key and how we talk about that collaboration matters.

829
00:31:10,870 --> 00:31:15,200
Effective team communication is, well, it's the engine room, as you said.

830
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:15,840
Exactly.

831
00:31:16,340 --> 00:31:18,560
So let's get foundational.

832
00:31:18,580 --> 00:31:22,850
One of the basic verbs we use when talking about teams, managing them, creating them.

833
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:23,140
Right.

834
00:31:23,400 --> 00:31:23,710
Okay.

835
00:31:23,790 --> 00:31:27,050
So first up, you've got to run a team or to lead a team.

836
00:31:27,750 --> 00:31:31,010
These sound similar, but they imply active management direction.

837
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:33,750
So it's not just being part of the team, it's guiding it.

838
00:31:33,990 --> 00:31:34,390
Precisely.

839
00:31:34,750 --> 00:31:43,480
If you say, I run a small team of software developers, or I lead one, you're stating you're the one responsible, setting direction, making sure things get done.

840
00:31:43,700 --> 00:31:44,440
Accountability.

841
00:31:44,580 --> 00:31:45,020
That's the word.

842
00:31:45,660 --> 00:31:52,010
And, you know, lead sometimes has a slightly more maybe inspirational feel, less purely administrative than run.

843
00:31:52,570 --> 00:31:53,360
Interesting nuance.

844
00:31:53,410 --> 00:31:55,400
Okay, so that's managing an existing team.

845
00:31:55,560 --> 00:31:57,960
What about starting one, like for a new project?

846
00:31:58,160 --> 00:31:59,640
Ah, then you build a team.

847
00:32:00,070 --> 00:32:04,410
This is really about assembling, creating a new group, often very strategically.

848
00:32:04,510 --> 00:32:05,860
Right, picking the right people.

849
00:32:06,100 --> 00:32:06,540
Exactly.

850
00:32:07,140 --> 00:32:08,940
Think about a new CEO coming in.

851
00:32:09,460 --> 00:32:13,850
Often, their first big task is to build a new senior management team.

852
00:32:15,050 --> 00:32:15,190
Mm-hmm.

853
00:32:15,270 --> 00:32:16,900
It's not just filling slots.

854
00:32:17,540 --> 00:32:18,220
It's deliberate.

855
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:24,410
Finding the right skills, the right experience mix, even the right personalities to work together.

856
00:32:24,430 --> 00:32:26,730
Or like building with blocks, making sure they fit.

857
00:32:26,750 --> 00:32:27,810
Yeah, a bit like that.

858
00:32:28,190 --> 00:32:32,640
The success of a new venture often hangs on how well that initial team is built.

859
00:32:33,040 --> 00:32:34,740
It's strategic resource allocation, really.

860
00:32:34,850 --> 00:32:35,650
Makes sense.

861
00:32:35,670 --> 00:32:36,930
And teams aren't static.

862
00:32:37,390 --> 00:32:38,310
People move around.

863
00:32:38,530 --> 00:32:38,810
Correct.

864
00:32:39,270 --> 00:32:42,380
So we need to join a team, pretty straightforward, becoming part of a group.

865
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:45,010
Like when you start a new job or move departments.

866
00:32:45,090 --> 00:32:45,550
Exactly.

867
00:32:45,870 --> 00:32:49,080
And the opposite is to leave a team, to depart from it.

868
00:32:49,240 --> 00:32:49,460
Okay.

869
00:32:49,480 --> 00:32:53,850
Like the example, Susan is moving to a different department next month, so she will be leaving the team.

870
00:32:54,010 --> 00:32:54,190
Right.

871
00:32:54,790 --> 00:33:00,200
These just describe the natural flow, the changes within an organization, career moves, project ends, that sort of thing.

872
00:33:00,460 --> 00:33:00,900
Got it.

873
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:02,640
So those are the core actions.

874
00:33:03,080 --> 00:33:05,220
Now, how do we describe these teams?

875
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:07,350
What adjectives give us a quick snapshot?

876
00:33:07,370 --> 00:33:08,030
Good question.

877
00:33:08,610 --> 00:33:10,350
Adjectives are shortcuts, right?

878
00:33:10,610 --> 00:33:11,710
They set expectations.

879
00:33:12,240 --> 00:33:14,740
You can have a successful team, one that hits its goals.

880
00:33:14,980 --> 00:33:15,560
They deliver.

881
00:33:15,920 --> 00:33:16,420
They deliver.

882
00:33:16,880 --> 00:33:20,650
Then there's a winning team, which sounds a bit more competitive, maybe.

883
00:33:20,630 --> 00:33:23,810
Consistently achieving victories, outperforming others.

884
00:33:23,910 --> 00:33:24,070
Right.

885
00:33:24,110 --> 00:33:26,760
Maybe in sales or sports contexts more.

886
00:33:27,140 --> 00:33:27,420
Often.

887
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:27,820
Yeah.

888
00:33:28,360 --> 00:33:30,640
And then very commonly an experienced team.

889
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:35,830
This means the members have significant background, knowledge, skills.

890
00:33:35,850 --> 00:33:36,550
Like the example.

891
00:33:36,890 --> 00:33:39,590
Most of the people in our group have been working in the industry for years.

892
00:33:39,670 --> 00:33:41,070
It's a very experienced team.

893
00:33:41,170 --> 00:33:41,710
Precisely.

894
00:33:42,360 --> 00:33:46,500
That immediately tells you something about their likely capability, their reliability.

895
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:48,120
It conveys competence.

896
00:33:48,220 --> 00:33:51,400
So verbs for actions, adjectives for descriptions.

897
00:33:51,440 --> 00:33:54,490
What about nouns, things related to the concept of teamwork?

898
00:33:54,630 --> 00:33:55,830
Right, the collective aspect.

899
00:33:56,130 --> 00:33:57,770
First, team effort.

900
00:33:57,790 --> 00:34:00,020
This highlights that success wasn't down to one person.

901
00:34:00,100 --> 00:34:01,020
Shared achievement.

902
00:34:01,040 --> 00:34:01,440
Exactly.

903
00:34:01,920 --> 00:34:04,120
I wasn't the only one who made this project a success.

904
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:05,040
It was a team effort.

905
00:34:05,640 --> 00:34:09,270
It promotes that idea of shared credit, which is important for morale.

906
00:34:09,330 --> 00:34:09,910
Definitely.

907
00:34:10,650 --> 00:34:12,920
And what about the individual within that team?

908
00:34:13,280 --> 00:34:14,340
That's your team player.

909
00:34:14,900 --> 00:34:18,450
Someone who works well with others, collaborates, supports the group's goals.

910
00:34:18,610 --> 00:34:19,430
Highly valued.

911
00:34:19,730 --> 00:34:20,170
Hugely.

912
00:34:20,810 --> 00:34:21,900
The contrast makes it clear.

913
00:34:22,540 --> 00:34:23,560
He prefers working alone.

914
00:34:23,880 --> 00:34:24,720
He's not a team player.

915
00:34:25,240 --> 00:34:25,620
No.

916
00:34:25,640 --> 00:34:29,650
In most places, being a team player is seen as a major asset.

917
00:34:29,630 --> 00:34:34,060
And that feeling, that positive vibe you get with some teams.

918
00:34:34,640 --> 00:34:39,680
That's team spirit, that sort of enthusiastic camaraderie, the feeling of being in it together.

919
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:43,710
Like I love working in our department because there's such a great sense of team spirit.

920
00:34:43,790 --> 00:34:44,050
Yes.

921
00:34:44,810 --> 00:34:45,730
And it's not just fluff.

922
00:34:46,210 --> 00:34:48,580
Good team spirit boosts productivity, resilience.

923
00:34:48,980 --> 00:34:51,160
People feel safer, more willing to contribute.

924
00:34:51,200 --> 00:34:52,020
It's a real asset.

925
00:34:52,340 --> 00:34:52,820
Absolutely.

926
00:34:53,340 --> 00:34:57,030
And companies try to foster it through team building activities designed for that purpose.

927
00:34:57,050 --> 00:34:57,210
Right.

928
00:34:57,230 --> 00:34:58,930
Those away days or workshops.

929
00:34:58,910 --> 00:34:59,450
Exactly.

930
00:34:59,790 --> 00:35:07,050
And, of course, you have the team leader, the person in charge, which loops back to run or lead a team.

931
00:35:08,110 --> 00:35:09,550
Paul is one of our best team leaders.

932
00:35:10,190 --> 00:35:11,210
It identifies the role.

933
00:35:11,310 --> 00:35:11,610
Okay.

934
00:35:11,630 --> 00:35:14,820
That gives us a really solid base for talking about teams.

935
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:18,450
Now let's get into the day-to-day, those quick interactions.

936
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:20,750
You mentioned three key phrases.

937
00:35:21,220 --> 00:35:27,840
Yes, these are incredibly useful for keeping things moving smoothly without needing a formal meeting for everything.

938
00:35:28,910 --> 00:35:30,310
First, touch base.

939
00:35:30,720 --> 00:35:31,540
Heard that one a lot.

940
00:35:31,600 --> 00:35:32,560
What does it really mean?

941
00:35:32,630 --> 00:35:37,790
It means talking to someone, usually briefly, just to catch up on something, see what's happening, get an update.

942
00:35:38,130 --> 00:35:39,530
It's informal but professional.

943
00:35:39,550 --> 00:35:40,290
Like a quick check-in.

944
00:35:40,570 --> 00:35:41,010
Exactly.

945
00:35:41,470 --> 00:35:43,660
The boss said he wants to touch base with you tomorrow.

946
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:46,620
It's about staying aligned, making sure you're on the same page.

947
00:35:47,060 --> 00:35:48,560
Prevents things falling through the cracks.

948
00:35:48,580 --> 00:35:49,540
Okay, proactive.

949
00:35:49,560 --> 00:35:51,120
And if I'm the one who needs to give updates?

950
00:35:51,300 --> 00:35:52,430
Then you keep someone posted.

951
00:35:52,910 --> 00:35:55,570
You tell them you'll regularly give them the latest news on something.

952
00:35:55,590 --> 00:35:57,590
So I'll keep you posted on the project.

953
00:35:57,930 --> 00:35:58,630
Progress.

954
00:35:58,920 --> 00:35:59,160
Right.

955
00:35:59,500 --> 00:36:02,280
It shows transparency, manages expectations.

956
00:36:02,620 --> 00:36:03,680
People know you'll update them.

957
00:36:03,700 --> 00:36:04,640
They don't have to chase you.

958
00:36:04,910 --> 00:36:05,670
Builds confidence.

959
00:36:06,030 --> 00:36:06,450
Good one.

960
00:36:07,410 --> 00:36:09,890
And the third for brainstorming.

961
00:36:10,290 --> 00:36:12,270
That's to bounce ideas off someone.

962
00:36:12,530 --> 00:36:16,560
You talk about your early ideas with someone to get their quick opinion, their feedback.

963
00:36:16,600 --> 00:36:18,120
Before you fully form them.

964
00:36:18,140 --> 00:36:18,940
Yeah, exactly.

965
00:36:18,960 --> 00:36:19,760
It's low pressure.

966
00:36:20,300 --> 00:36:22,610
I'd like to bounce some ideas off you if you have a few minutes.

967
00:36:23,150 --> 00:36:25,610
It invites collaboration, feedback.

968
00:36:25,630 --> 00:36:27,250
Helps refine things early.

969
00:36:27,350 --> 00:36:27,910
Definitely.

970
00:36:28,340 --> 00:36:29,180
Saves time later.

971
00:36:29,240 --> 00:36:32,120
You test the water, see if an idea has legs.

972
00:36:32,890 --> 00:36:34,350
It's great for collaborative environments.

973
00:36:34,610 --> 00:36:35,050
Brilliant.

974
00:36:35,370 --> 00:36:38,420
So touch base, keep someone posted, bounce ideas off.

975
00:36:38,740 --> 00:36:40,830
Really practical stuff for daily teamwork.

976
00:36:40,970 --> 00:36:43,010
They really facilitate smooth communication.

977
00:36:43,150 --> 00:36:54,000
So wrapping up this section, we've got the verbs for leading and joining, adjectives like successful or experienced, nouns like team effort and team spirit, and these key interaction phrases.

978
00:36:54,440 --> 00:36:56,910
It really paints a picture of how essential this language is.

979
00:36:57,260 --> 00:36:57,970
It really does.

980
00:36:58,350 --> 00:37:02,250
Precision here makes a huge difference in how effectively teams operate.

981
00:37:02,450 --> 00:37:02,790
Okay.

982
00:37:03,150 --> 00:37:07,900
And speaking of operating effectively, let's move to another cornerstone of business life.

983
00:37:08,560 --> 00:37:09,020
Meetings.

984
00:37:09,980 --> 00:37:10,470
Ah, yes.

985
00:37:11,370 --> 00:37:11,930
The meeting.

986
00:37:12,750 --> 00:37:13,470
Can't escape them.

987
00:37:13,730 --> 00:37:14,330
Nope.

988
00:37:14,350 --> 00:37:15,910
Love them or hate them, they're everywhere.

989
00:37:15,930 --> 00:37:21,060
And again, the language we use around them can make a big difference between productivity and frustration.

990
00:37:21,340 --> 00:37:21,800
Absolutely.

991
00:37:22,180 --> 00:37:28,700
Knowing the right terms helps set expectations, clarifies roles, ensures everyone's on the same page from the start.

992
00:37:28,680 --> 00:37:29,820
Let's start with the verbs again.

993
00:37:29,840 --> 00:37:32,660
You mentioned formal versus less formal options.

994
00:37:32,740 --> 00:37:33,120
That's right.

995
00:37:33,580 --> 00:37:34,610
Take just being present.

996
00:37:35,090 --> 00:37:37,130
Formally, you attend a meeting.

997
00:37:38,030 --> 00:37:40,800
The CEO would like everyone to attend the next meeting.

998
00:37:41,400 --> 00:37:42,340
Sounds quite official.

999
00:37:42,480 --> 00:37:42,820
Okay.

1000
00:37:42,840 --> 00:37:43,500
And less formal.

1001
00:37:43,520 --> 00:37:44,360
You just go to a meeting.

1002
00:37:44,960 --> 00:37:46,610
The boss wants everyone to go to the next meeting.

1003
00:37:46,680 --> 00:37:49,570
Same meaning, just a bit more casual, maybe for routine meetings.

1004
00:37:49,550 --> 00:37:51,030
Attend versus go to.

1005
00:37:51,270 --> 00:37:51,870
Simple enough.

1006
00:37:52,030 --> 00:37:54,540
What about actively contributing, not just sitting there?

1007
00:37:54,680 --> 00:37:55,080
Good point.

1008
00:37:55,560 --> 00:37:57,240
Formally, you participate in the meeting.

1009
00:37:57,460 --> 00:37:58,880
Did everyone participate in the meeting?

1010
00:37:59,320 --> 00:38:01,220
This emphasizes active involvement.

1011
00:38:01,460 --> 00:38:03,590
Asking questions, sharing ideas.

1012
00:38:03,970 --> 00:38:04,410
Exactly.

1013
00:38:04,830 --> 00:38:07,090
Less formally, you take part in the meeting.

1014
00:38:07,190 --> 00:38:08,510
Did everyone take part in the meeting?

1015
00:38:09,010 --> 00:38:13,820
Again, same meaning, but emphasizes contribution over just being physically present.

1016
00:38:14,680 --> 00:38:15,960
Active engagement is key.

1017
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:18,300
Definitely valued more than just warming a seat.

1018
00:38:18,920 --> 00:38:19,020
Okay.

1019
00:38:19,040 --> 00:38:20,000
Who organizes it?

1020
00:38:20,140 --> 00:38:21,470
Formally, you hold a meeting.

1021
00:38:22,070 --> 00:38:24,310
They hold a meeting to discuss the project's future.

1022
00:38:24,810 --> 00:38:25,670
Suggests a planned event.

1023
00:38:26,010 --> 00:38:27,130
And less formally.

1024
00:38:27,150 --> 00:38:28,170
You just have a meeting.

1025
00:38:28,530 --> 00:38:29,280
They had a meeting.

1026
00:38:29,660 --> 00:38:32,020
It identifies who initiated, who's running the show.

1027
00:38:32,080 --> 00:38:32,340
Right.

1028
00:38:32,480 --> 00:38:36,320
And the person actually in charge during a meeting, guiding the discussion.

1029
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:38,950
Formally, that person will chair a meeting.

1030
00:38:39,350 --> 00:38:43,790
Ms. Linda will chair the next meeting, often used for more structured official meetings.

1031
00:38:43,830 --> 00:38:44,740
Like board meetings.

1032
00:38:44,820 --> 00:38:45,180
Exactly.

1033
00:38:45,220 --> 00:38:45,700
Or committees.

1034
00:38:46,160 --> 00:38:49,940
More commonly, less formally, they run a meeting or lead a meeting.

1035
00:38:50,820 --> 00:38:53,710
Ms. Linda will run the next meeting or lead it.

1036
00:38:54,210 --> 00:38:57,530
So chair, run, lead all mean being in charge.

1037
00:38:57,590 --> 00:38:57,790
Yes.

1038
00:38:58,070 --> 00:39:01,360
Guiding the discussion, managing time, ensuring things stay on track.

1039
00:39:01,880 --> 00:39:02,680
A crucial role.

1040
00:39:02,900 --> 00:39:03,240
No doubt.

1041
00:39:03,380 --> 00:39:05,500
And inevitably, sometimes meetings get rescheduled.

1042
00:39:06,140 --> 00:39:06,900
Happens all the time.

1043
00:39:08,200 --> 00:39:08,760
Formally.

1044
00:39:09,170 --> 00:39:10,210
You postpone a meeting.

1045
00:39:11,010 --> 00:39:14,400
Unfortunately, we have to postpone the meeting until next week.

1046
00:39:14,420 --> 00:39:15,920
Sounds professional, clear.

1047
00:39:16,060 --> 00:39:16,420
It is.

1048
00:39:17,100 --> 00:39:18,790
Less formally, you can put off a meeting.

1049
00:39:19,230 --> 00:39:20,830
Sorry, but we have to put off the meeting.

1050
00:39:21,510 --> 00:39:23,460
Same outcome, just slightly more casual phrasing.

1051
00:39:23,880 --> 00:39:25,980
And clear communication is key there, right?

1052
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:27,750
Tell people the new plan.

1053
00:39:27,880 --> 00:39:29,000
Absolutely essential.

1054
00:39:29,600 --> 00:39:35,440
State the reason briefly, if appropriate, provide the new time, or say when you will provide it.

1055
00:39:36,060 --> 00:39:36,940
Avoids confusion.

1056
00:39:37,140 --> 00:39:38,470
Okay, that covers the main verbs.

1057
00:39:38,590 --> 00:39:39,330
Let's broaden out.

1058
00:39:39,650 --> 00:39:42,400
What about adjectives describing different types of meetings?

1059
00:39:42,780 --> 00:39:43,020
Right.

1060
00:39:43,080 --> 00:39:44,120
Context is important.

1061
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:46,050
A brief meeting is just a short one.

1062
00:39:46,850 --> 00:39:47,930
It'll be a brief meeting.

1063
00:39:48,570 --> 00:39:50,550
Signals efficiency, respect for time.

1064
00:39:50,570 --> 00:39:51,580
Good for quick updates.

1065
00:39:52,040 --> 00:39:52,480
Exactly.

1066
00:39:53,180 --> 00:39:57,120
Then you have an urgent meeting called when something needs immediate attention.

1067
00:39:57,770 --> 00:40:00,030
They held an urgent meeting to discuss the crisis.

1068
00:40:00,610 --> 00:40:02,820
Implies high priority, immediate action needed.

1069
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:05,060
Okay, brief for speed, urgent for crisis.

1070
00:40:05,240 --> 00:40:06,260
What about projects?

1071
00:40:06,280 --> 00:40:10,180
You often have a follow-up meeting, held later to check progress after an earlier one.

1072
00:40:10,530 --> 00:40:11,710
Let's arrange a follow-up meeting.

1073
00:40:12,330 --> 00:40:14,330
Keeps momentum, ensures accountability.

1074
00:40:14,690 --> 00:40:16,490
Makes sense, like checking on action items.

1075
00:40:16,590 --> 00:40:16,990
Precisely.

1076
00:40:17,730 --> 00:40:21,740
And very importantly, the kickoff meeting, usually the official start of a project.

1077
00:40:21,940 --> 00:40:23,220
Getting everyone aligned at the beginning.

1078
00:40:23,260 --> 00:40:23,840
Yes.

1079
00:40:24,160 --> 00:40:26,520
The whole project team attended the kickoff meeting.

1080
00:40:26,720 --> 00:40:31,610
It sets the goals, roles, expectations foundational for project success.

1081
00:40:31,830 --> 00:40:35,380
These adjectives really frame the meeting's purpose nicely.

1082
00:40:35,550 --> 00:40:39,440
What about the administrative side, the documents?

1083
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:40,220
Ah, yes.

1084
00:40:40,960 --> 00:40:42,310
Crucial for formal meetings.

1085
00:40:42,770 --> 00:40:45,210
You have the agenda, the official list of topics.

1086
00:40:45,850 --> 00:40:47,490
Has everyone received the agenda?

1087
00:40:47,830 --> 00:40:49,600
Essential for preparation and structure.

1088
00:40:49,760 --> 00:40:50,940
The roadmap for the meeting.

1089
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:51,600
Exactly.

1090
00:40:51,950 --> 00:40:54,560
And each point on that list is an item on the agenda.

1091
00:40:55,180 --> 00:40:58,240
Let's move on to the last item on the agenda, guides the flow.

1092
00:40:58,500 --> 00:40:58,800
Okay.

1093
00:40:59,120 --> 00:41:00,290
And the record of what happened?

1094
00:41:00,490 --> 00:41:02,130
Yeah, it's taking the minutes.

1095
00:41:02,150 --> 00:41:06,080
Creating the official record, decisions made, actions assigned, deadlines.

1096
00:41:06,100 --> 00:41:07,000
Tom will take the minutes.

1097
00:41:07,320 --> 00:41:09,040
Not just notes, but the official record.

1098
00:41:09,060 --> 00:41:09,360
Correct.

1099
00:41:09,660 --> 00:41:11,590
For accountability, reference later on.

1100
00:41:11,750 --> 00:41:12,730
It's the meeting's memory.

1101
00:41:12,880 --> 00:41:13,280
Wow.

1102
00:41:13,500 --> 00:41:13,800
Okay.

1103
00:41:14,140 --> 00:41:23,290
That's a really thorough look at meeting language from attending and sharing to different types like kickoff or follow up and the agenda and minutes.

1104
00:41:24,010 --> 00:41:27,480
It shows how using the right words brings clarity and professionalism.

1105
00:41:27,720 --> 00:41:28,680
It absolutely does.

1106
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:32,390
Precision prevents ambiguity and saves time.

1107
00:41:32,670 --> 00:41:33,910
So what does this all mean?

1108
00:41:33,930 --> 00:41:40,940
It means having this vocabulary lets you navigate meetings much more effectively, whether you're running them or just participating.

1109
00:41:41,120 --> 00:41:41,570
Exactly.

1110
00:41:42,350 --> 00:41:51,220
And building on that idea of effective interaction, let's dive into the actual discourse, how we express opinions, agree and importantly, disagree in a professional way.

1111
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:52,160
Ah, yes.

1112
00:41:52,720 --> 00:41:53,740
The art of dialogue.

1113
00:41:54,060 --> 00:41:56,640
Crucial for collaboration and avoiding misunderstandings.

1114
00:41:56,760 --> 00:41:57,760
Paramount.

1115
00:41:57,780 --> 00:42:01,450
Getting this right fosters productive discussion, leads to better decisions.

1116
00:42:01,860 --> 00:42:05,070
It's about being clear and respectful, even when you don't see eye to eye.

1117
00:42:05,190 --> 00:42:05,390
Right.

1118
00:42:05,690 --> 00:42:06,990
Let's start with asking for opinions.

1119
00:42:07,330 --> 00:42:12,470
What do you think is fine, but sometimes you want something a bit more substantial.

1120
00:42:12,590 --> 00:42:13,220
Definitely.

1121
00:42:13,240 --> 00:42:13,920
You can elevate it.

1122
00:42:14,100 --> 00:42:16,540
Try, what are your thoughts on, like, what are your thoughts on the new strategy?

1123
00:42:16,940 --> 00:42:18,860
It invites a more considerate response.

1124
00:42:19,130 --> 00:42:20,250
Sounds more open-ended.

1125
00:42:20,650 --> 00:42:20,950
It is.

1126
00:42:21,310 --> 00:42:24,920
Or how do you feel about, for example, how do you feel about flexible working?

1127
00:42:25,580 --> 00:42:28,950
This acknowledges the subjective, maybe emotional side.

1128
00:42:29,010 --> 00:42:30,990
Good for topics that affect people personally.

1129
00:42:31,010 --> 00:42:31,470
Exactly.

1130
00:42:31,710 --> 00:42:35,440
And do you have any views on this marketing approach?

1131
00:42:36,040 --> 00:42:41,490
It subtly suggests you're looking for a perhaps more informed or specific perspective.

1132
00:42:41,610 --> 00:42:42,910
Those are great alternatives.

1133
00:42:43,370 --> 00:42:44,190
More engaging.

1134
00:42:44,710 --> 00:42:48,600
Now, expressing our opinions beyond just my opinion is...

1135
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:50,730
Yes, several options add nuance.

1136
00:42:51,370 --> 00:42:55,160
The way I see it offers your perspective based on your understanding.

1137
00:42:55,840 --> 00:42:57,720
The way I see it, we need more training.

1138
00:42:57,760 --> 00:42:58,860
Based on your viewpoint.

1139
00:42:58,900 --> 00:42:59,080
Right.

1140
00:42:59,620 --> 00:43:02,550
As far as I'm concerned, implies a stronger personal judgment or belief.

1141
00:43:03,110 --> 00:43:04,930
As far as I'm concerned, marks the best fit.

1142
00:43:05,290 --> 00:43:06,170
Shows conviction.

1143
00:43:06,190 --> 00:43:06,470
Okay.

1144
00:43:06,770 --> 00:43:07,490
Bit more weight there.

1145
00:43:07,760 --> 00:43:10,940
And in my view, is direct, professional.

1146
00:43:11,300 --> 00:43:12,850
In my view, the project was a success.

1147
00:43:13,110 --> 00:43:14,330
Good for formal assessments.

1148
00:43:14,680 --> 00:43:15,380
Clean and clear.

1149
00:43:16,040 --> 00:43:18,810
What if you feel really strongly you're totally convinced?

1150
00:43:19,090 --> 00:43:21,820
For strong opinions, you need impactful phrases.

1151
00:43:22,700 --> 00:43:24,950
I'm absolutely convinced that leaves no doubt.

1152
00:43:25,030 --> 00:43:27,060
I'm absolutely convinced this will work.

1153
00:43:27,080 --> 00:43:27,980
High confidence.

1154
00:43:28,250 --> 00:43:29,410
Okay, very certain.

1155
00:43:29,530 --> 00:43:33,110
Similarly, I strongly believe that indicates deep conviction.

1156
00:43:33,920 --> 00:43:38,960
I strongly believe we can hit these targets, often tied to principles or deep analysis.

1157
00:43:39,180 --> 00:43:40,390
A firm stance.

1158
00:43:40,850 --> 00:43:42,910
And I have no doubt that.

1159
00:43:43,190 --> 00:43:46,270
Remember, the silent B in doubt expresses complete certainty.

1160
00:43:46,950 --> 00:43:48,820
I have no doubt Alina will sort it out.

1161
00:43:49,500 --> 00:43:53,990
Use these when you need to be definitive, but maybe sparingly to keep their power.

1162
00:43:54,080 --> 00:43:54,690
Makes sense.

1163
00:43:54,990 --> 00:43:56,390
Use them when you really mean it.

1164
00:43:56,740 --> 00:43:58,360
Okay, that covers expressing ourselves.

1165
00:43:58,400 --> 00:43:59,210
What about responding?

1166
00:43:59,650 --> 00:44:01,480
Agreeing and disagreeing politely.

1167
00:44:01,740 --> 00:44:02,670
Crucial skills.

1168
00:44:03,250 --> 00:44:04,190
Let's start with agreeing.

1169
00:44:04,810 --> 00:44:06,570
Beyond I agree, you can add nuance.

1170
00:44:06,990 --> 00:44:09,910
For simple agreement, say a colleague suggests something.

1171
00:44:10,110 --> 00:44:11,180
Like hiring an agency.

1172
00:44:11,480 --> 00:44:11,640
Right.

1173
00:44:11,680 --> 00:44:16,980
You could say, absolutely, strong, enthusiastic, or that makes sense, showing you follow the logic.

1174
00:44:17,280 --> 00:44:19,410
Or I'd go along with that, indicating support.

1175
00:44:19,530 --> 00:44:20,430
Different shades of yes.

1176
00:44:20,550 --> 00:44:20,990
Exactly.

1177
00:44:21,250 --> 00:44:24,870
And for strong agreement, if someone says the IT system must be upgraded.

1178
00:44:24,950 --> 00:44:25,890
And you completely agree.

1179
00:44:26,050 --> 00:44:27,200
You could say, yes.

1180
00:44:27,180 --> 00:44:28,340
I couldn't agree more.

1181
00:44:28,760 --> 00:44:29,640
Total agreement.

1182
00:44:30,410 --> 00:44:31,750
Or you're absolutely right.

1183
00:44:32,650 --> 00:44:34,220
Validating their point strongly.

1184
00:44:34,580 --> 00:44:36,350
Or even that's exactly what I wanted to say.

1185
00:44:36,390 --> 00:44:37,230
Perfect alignment.

1186
00:44:37,550 --> 00:44:39,820
These really amplify your support.

1187
00:44:40,570 --> 00:44:44,060
Good for building consensus, but what about partial agreement?

1188
00:44:44,380 --> 00:44:47,470
You agree with the problem, maybe, but not the solution.

1189
00:44:47,670 --> 00:44:48,930
Very common scenario.

1190
00:44:48,950 --> 00:44:50,390
This requires careful phrasing.

1191
00:44:51,060 --> 00:44:52,920
Say someone suggests laying off 50 people.

1192
00:44:53,280 --> 00:44:56,170
You agree costs need cutting, but not that drastically.

1193
00:44:56,330 --> 00:44:57,310
Tricky situation.

1194
00:44:57,390 --> 00:45:00,140
You could say, I agree with you up to a point, but...

1195
00:45:00,490 --> 00:45:02,270
Do you really think we need to lay off so many?

1196
00:45:02,630 --> 00:45:04,890
Acknowledges validity, then raises the issue.

1197
00:45:04,980 --> 00:45:05,920
Polite challenge.

1198
00:45:06,300 --> 00:45:09,910
Or, I see where you're coming from, but I don't think that number is necessary.

1199
00:45:10,310 --> 00:45:13,490
Shows you understand their perspective first, then disagree gently.

1200
00:45:14,050 --> 00:45:15,260
These keep the dialogue open.

1201
00:45:15,300 --> 00:45:17,200
So vital for constructive debate.

1202
00:45:17,240 --> 00:45:18,750
Now the big one, disagreeing.

1203
00:45:19,150 --> 00:45:20,870
How to do it without causing offense.

1204
00:45:20,990 --> 00:45:24,090
Politeness is key here, often using softeners like I'm afraid.

1205
00:45:24,130 --> 00:45:29,980
If you think a budget increase proposal is wrong, you could say, I'm afraid I can't go along with that.

1206
00:45:30,500 --> 00:45:31,880
Polite but firm refusal.

1207
00:45:32,420 --> 00:45:36,350
Or I'm not so sure about that, expresses doubt gently.

1208
00:45:37,310 --> 00:45:39,670
Or even softer, I'm not sure I would agree with that.

1209
00:45:39,860 --> 00:45:41,680
These create space for discussion, right?

1210
00:45:41,840 --> 00:45:42,940
Not just shutting it down.

1211
00:45:42,960 --> 00:45:43,460
Exactly.

1212
00:45:44,040 --> 00:45:46,750
They signal disagreement without being confrontational.

1213
00:45:47,230 --> 00:45:48,290
Preserves the relationship.

1214
00:45:48,670 --> 00:45:48,950
Okay.

1215
00:45:49,570 --> 00:45:54,940
But are there times for strong disagreement when something is just plain wrong or unacceptable?

1216
00:45:55,420 --> 00:45:57,530
Yes, but use with caution.

1217
00:45:58,200 --> 00:46:00,400
depends heavily on context and relationship.

1218
00:46:01,100 --> 00:46:05,630
Say a partner suggests something truly damaging, like cancelling a key contract suddenly.

1219
00:46:05,650 --> 00:46:06,050
By stakes.

1220
00:46:06,070 --> 00:46:09,250
You might react with, you can't be serious, expresses shock.

1221
00:46:09,710 --> 00:46:11,560
Or, I'm sorry, but that's totally unacceptable.

1222
00:46:11,920 --> 00:46:12,980
Very strong rejection.

1223
00:46:13,360 --> 00:46:15,340
Or, I'm sorry, but there's no way I can accept that.

1224
00:46:15,660 --> 00:46:16,400
Absolute refusal.

1225
00:46:16,520 --> 00:46:17,160
Wow, okay.

1226
00:46:17,180 --> 00:46:18,280
Those are very direct.

1227
00:46:18,550 --> 00:46:18,910
They are.

1228
00:46:19,390 --> 00:46:20,390
They signal a hard line.

1229
00:46:20,670 --> 00:46:27,720
For situations where ambiguity is dangerous, but again, used sparingly and appropriately, they can escalate things quickly if misused.

1230
00:46:27,830 --> 00:46:29,440
Here's where it gets really interesting.

1231
00:46:29,460 --> 00:46:33,430
This whole spectrum, it's not just yes, no, agree, disagree.

1232
00:46:33,450 --> 00:46:38,140
It's about choosing the right level of intensity and politeness for the situation.

1233
00:46:38,180 --> 00:46:40,590
Nuance is power.

1234
00:46:40,790 --> 00:46:41,590
Beautifully put.

1235
00:46:41,730 --> 00:46:43,280
It's strategic communication.

1236
00:46:43,470 --> 00:46:47,460
And that leads us nicely into managing the flow of communication.

1237
00:46:47,520 --> 00:46:49,140
Interruptions happen.

1238
00:46:49,200 --> 00:46:50,590
How do we handle them?

1239
00:46:50,750 --> 00:46:53,050
And then how do we talk about problems effectively?

1240
00:46:53,090 --> 00:46:53,830
Exactly.

1241
00:46:54,170 --> 00:46:58,900
Managing the conversational traffic, especially in meetings, and then articulating challenges clearly.

1242
00:46:59,620 --> 00:47:00,500
Both vital skills.

1243
00:47:00,930 --> 00:47:02,190
Let's tackle interruptions first.

1244
00:47:02,730 --> 00:47:06,960
How can you politely interject if you need to clarify something or add a point quickly?

1245
00:47:07,000 --> 00:47:08,960
Cutting someone off feels rude.

1246
00:47:09,090 --> 00:47:11,070
It can, but sometimes it's necessary.

1247
00:47:11,090 --> 00:47:12,260
Blight phrases help.

1248
00:47:12,280 --> 00:47:15,480
Before you go on, could you is good for immediate clarification.

1249
00:47:15,580 --> 00:47:17,780
Before you go on, could you clarify the budget figure?

1250
00:47:18,140 --> 00:47:18,260
Okay.

1251
00:47:18,280 --> 00:47:20,350
Targets a specific need before they continue?

1252
00:47:20,550 --> 00:47:20,770
Yes.

1253
00:47:21,510 --> 00:47:22,890
Or can I just add something here?

1254
00:47:23,470 --> 00:47:25,750
Use this when you have a quick, relevant piece of info.

1255
00:47:25,770 --> 00:47:26,720
Can I just add something here?

1256
00:47:26,780 --> 00:47:27,960
I checked those numbers yesterday.

1257
00:47:28,120 --> 00:47:29,780
Contributing relevant info right away.

1258
00:47:30,140 --> 00:47:30,300
Right.

1259
00:47:30,580 --> 00:47:31,940
Can I just stop you there?

1260
00:47:32,230 --> 00:47:35,030
is often for a point of disagreement or immediate correction.

1261
00:47:36,170 --> 00:47:36,930
Can I just stop you there?

1262
00:47:37,230 --> 00:47:38,860
I think the deadline is actually next week.

1263
00:47:39,200 --> 00:47:40,000
A bit more direct.

1264
00:47:40,400 --> 00:47:40,620
It is.

1265
00:47:41,220 --> 00:47:49,210
And, sorry for interrupting, but is the classic polite apology before making your point, especially if it's a slightly longer interjection.

1266
00:47:49,750 --> 00:47:52,980
Sorry for interrupting, but we need to consider the resource allocation.

1267
00:47:53,300 --> 00:47:54,100
Shows awareness.

1268
00:47:54,360 --> 00:47:56,280
Okay, so that's how to interrupt politely.

1269
00:47:56,320 --> 00:47:58,200
What about when you're interrupted?

1270
00:47:58,520 --> 00:48:00,010
You need ways to manage it.

1271
00:48:00,730 --> 00:48:05,430
If you want to defer answering, I'll answer that question later if you don't mind, politely delays it.

1272
00:48:05,610 --> 00:48:06,460
Buys you time.

1273
00:48:07,080 --> 00:48:07,840
Keeps you on track.

1274
00:48:07,920 --> 00:48:08,180
Yes.

1275
00:48:08,660 --> 00:48:16,870
To get back to your point after the interruption, use, as I was saying, or as I was explaining, as I was saying, the main challenge is it smoothly brings the focus back.

1276
00:48:17,210 --> 00:48:18,670
Re-rails the conversation.

1277
00:48:18,790 --> 00:48:19,350
Exactly.

1278
00:48:20,070 --> 00:48:24,700
If you're okay with the interruption, a simple, yes, go ahead, works, shows you're listening.

1279
00:48:24,900 --> 00:48:25,900
Welcoming their input.

1280
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:26,160
Exactly.

1281
00:48:26,630 --> 00:48:29,800
But if you really need to finish your thought, let me finish, please.

1282
00:48:30,180 --> 00:48:31,940
It's firm, but polite.

1283
00:48:32,120 --> 00:48:33,140
Right.

1284
00:48:33,160 --> 00:48:34,130
Or slightly softer.

1285
00:48:34,270 --> 00:48:35,250
I'm almost finished my point.

1286
00:48:35,810 --> 00:48:37,290
Signals they jumped in a bit too soon.

1287
00:48:37,710 --> 00:48:38,410
Okay.

1288
00:48:38,430 --> 00:48:39,380
And if you get thrown off.

1289
00:48:39,660 --> 00:48:40,380
Happens to everyone.

1290
00:48:40,960 --> 00:48:43,120
Just use, where was I?

1291
00:48:43,340 --> 00:48:45,120
It's a natural way to pause and reorient.

1292
00:48:45,680 --> 00:48:46,260
Where was I?

1293
00:48:46,460 --> 00:48:48,710
Oh, yes, the implementation plan.

1294
00:48:49,150 --> 00:48:53,160
Super useful phrases for navigating real-time conversation dynamics.

1295
00:48:53,740 --> 00:48:58,850
Okay, from managing flow to talking about difficulties, problems are inevitable, right?

1296
00:48:59,090 --> 00:48:59,610
Absolutely.

1297
00:49:00,230 --> 00:49:03,060
And how we articulate them is the first step to solving them.

1298
00:49:03,480 --> 00:49:05,580
Clear problem statements are crucial.

1299
00:49:05,880 --> 00:49:09,110
So what are the basic ways to say we have a problem?

1300
00:49:09,150 --> 00:49:11,510
The very common structures involve have.

1301
00:49:12,010 --> 00:49:16,260
You can have problems with, have trouble with, have difficulties with, or have issues with something.

1302
00:49:16,480 --> 00:49:19,800
Like we're having problems, trouble, difficulty issues with the new system.

1303
00:49:20,020 --> 00:49:20,580
Exactly.

1304
00:49:20,960 --> 00:49:24,430
Just remember trouble is usually singular here while the others are plural.

1305
00:49:24,730 --> 00:49:26,110
They all signal a challenge.

1306
00:49:26,350 --> 00:49:29,810
Issues can sometimes feel a bit softer or more systemic.

1307
00:49:29,830 --> 00:49:30,270
Got it.

1308
00:49:30,660 --> 00:49:32,400
And describing the difficulty itself.

1309
00:49:32,430 --> 00:49:34,750
You can say, it's difficult hard to do something.

1310
00:49:34,790 --> 00:49:36,530
It's difficult to find good candidates.

1311
00:49:37,320 --> 00:49:39,600
Focuses on the task's inherent challenge.

1312
00:49:39,920 --> 00:49:41,000
Objective difficulty.

1313
00:49:41,280 --> 00:49:41,480
Right.

1314
00:49:42,320 --> 00:49:45,530
Or you can say, you find it difficult hard to do something.

1315
00:49:45,890 --> 00:49:47,610
We find it difficult to meet the deadline.

1316
00:49:48,430 --> 00:49:50,740
This focuses more on the experience of the difficulty.

1317
00:49:50,980 --> 00:49:52,640
Subjective experience.

1318
00:49:52,660 --> 00:49:52,760
Okay.

1319
00:49:53,400 --> 00:49:57,930
Now, once identified, how do we talk about addressing the problem, taking action?

1320
00:49:58,010 --> 00:50:00,800
We use verbs like tackle, address, or deal with.

1321
00:50:01,540 --> 00:50:02,780
They all mean taking action.

1322
00:50:02,960 --> 00:50:03,900
Proactive words.

1323
00:50:04,300 --> 00:50:04,890
Very much so.

1324
00:50:05,270 --> 00:50:06,350
We need to tackle this now.

1325
00:50:06,990 --> 00:50:07,710
Urgent action.

1326
00:50:08,090 --> 00:50:09,640
These are the issues we need to address.

1327
00:50:10,180 --> 00:50:11,000
Systematic approach.

1328
00:50:11,460 --> 00:50:13,440
She's great at dealing with complex problems.

1329
00:50:13,950 --> 00:50:14,830
Capability.

1330
00:50:14,850 --> 00:50:16,490
They signal a move towards resolution.

1331
00:50:16,470 --> 00:50:18,260
And problems vary in scale, right?

1332
00:50:18,380 --> 00:50:20,300
Minor hiccup versus major crisis.

1333
00:50:20,440 --> 00:50:20,780
Definitely.

1334
00:50:21,260 --> 00:50:22,130
Adjectives help here.

1335
00:50:22,570 --> 00:50:23,990
A minor problem isn't serious.

1336
00:50:24,410 --> 00:50:26,740
Some minor problems initially, but we fix them.

1337
00:50:26,760 --> 00:50:27,320
Low impact.

1338
00:50:27,480 --> 00:50:28,000
Manageable.

1339
00:50:28,080 --> 00:50:29,590
A major problem is...

1340
00:50:30,020 --> 00:50:30,660
Very serious.

1341
00:50:31,060 --> 00:50:32,890
This can become a major problem if we ignore it.

1342
00:50:33,170 --> 00:50:34,770
High stakes, needs attention.

1343
00:50:35,050 --> 00:50:35,330
Got it.

1344
00:50:35,590 --> 00:50:36,510
Minor versus major.

1345
00:50:36,570 --> 00:50:37,360
Any others?

1346
00:50:37,580 --> 00:50:37,800
Yes.

1347
00:50:37,940 --> 00:50:39,860
An immediate problem needs urgent action now.

1348
00:50:40,360 --> 00:50:42,050
Cash flow is an immediate problem.

1349
00:50:42,450 --> 00:50:43,910
And a persistent problem keeps happening.

1350
00:50:44,430 --> 00:50:48,840
Persistent problems with the server suggests an ongoing, maybe deeper issue.

1351
00:50:48,970 --> 00:50:50,950
That adds really useful granularity.

1352
00:50:51,010 --> 00:50:51,860
What about idioms?

1353
00:50:52,510 --> 00:50:54,380
Any colorful ways to talk about problems?

1354
00:50:54,480 --> 00:50:55,000
Oh, plenty.

1355
00:50:55,620 --> 00:50:59,740
If a situation comes to a head, it suddenly gets critical and needs immediate attention.

1356
00:51:00,820 --> 00:51:03,290
The situation came to a head when the client threatened to leave.

1357
00:51:04,030 --> 00:51:04,710
A crisis point.

1358
00:51:04,730 --> 00:51:05,610
The breaking point.

1359
00:51:05,710 --> 00:51:06,190
Exactly.

1360
00:51:06,770 --> 00:51:10,090
If a business is in dire straits, it's an extreme difficulty.

1361
00:51:10,650 --> 00:51:13,070
The pandemic left many firms in dire straits.

1362
00:51:13,900 --> 00:51:14,780
Severe trouble.

1363
00:51:14,840 --> 00:51:15,500
Sounds serious.

1364
00:51:15,580 --> 00:51:17,720
What about newer things, startup issues?

1365
00:51:17,740 --> 00:51:19,470
Ah, those are often called teething problems.

1366
00:51:19,990 --> 00:51:20,090
Yeah.

1367
00:51:20,110 --> 00:51:23,810
Initial difficulties when starting something new, usually expected and temporary.

1368
00:51:24,150 --> 00:51:26,900
Some teething problems with the new software, but it's better now.

1369
00:51:27,300 --> 00:51:28,260
Okay, normal startup bumps.

1370
00:51:28,740 --> 00:51:31,060
And a positive one, when things improve after a tough time.

1371
00:51:31,140 --> 00:51:33,040
That's when you see light at the end of the tunnel.

1372
00:51:33,060 --> 00:51:35,110
Signs of hope, improvement after difficulty.

1373
00:51:35,550 --> 00:51:39,510
Sales were bad, but with the new campaign, we finally see light at the end of the tunnel.

1374
00:51:39,950 --> 00:51:40,930
Optimism returns.

1375
00:51:41,070 --> 00:51:42,070
Love that one.

1376
00:51:42,110 --> 00:51:50,470
So from minor issues and teething problems to dire straits and seeing the light, a really rich way to describe the landscape of challenges.

1377
00:51:50,700 --> 00:51:56,470
It allows for precision and shared understanding, which is crucial for tackling those challenges effectively.

1378
00:51:56,670 --> 00:51:57,150
Absolutely.

1379
00:51:57,490 --> 00:52:01,980
Which brings us perfectly to our final section, the more strategic language of business.

1380
00:52:02,220 --> 00:52:08,290
Things like decisions, compensation, experience, the big picture stuff.

1381
00:52:08,350 --> 00:52:08,570
Right.

1382
00:52:08,630 --> 00:52:11,120
Moving into language that shapes strategy and careers.

1383
00:52:11,900 --> 00:52:12,980
Let's start with decisions.

1384
00:52:13,220 --> 00:52:14,580
They drive everything.

1385
00:52:14,610 --> 00:52:15,390
They really do.

1386
00:52:15,930 --> 00:52:17,840
And how we talk about decisions is key.

1387
00:52:18,200 --> 00:52:19,720
What are common word combinations?

1388
00:52:20,040 --> 00:52:21,440
We often qualify decision.

1389
00:52:21,940 --> 00:52:24,810
A key decision is extremely important, strategic.

1390
00:52:25,290 --> 00:52:29,110
The CEO makes key decisions about expansion, high impact.

1391
00:52:29,370 --> 00:52:30,160
Not trivial.

1392
00:52:30,260 --> 00:52:30,450
No.

1393
00:52:31,020 --> 00:52:33,640
A tough decision is difficult, involves trade-offs.

1394
00:52:34,020 --> 00:52:36,600
It was a tough decision to close that branch.

1395
00:52:36,580 --> 00:52:37,660
Conveys the challenge.

1396
00:52:37,740 --> 00:52:39,030
Acknowledges the difficulty.

1397
00:52:39,150 --> 00:52:41,770
A poor decision, obviously, is a bad one with negative outcomes.

1398
00:52:42,410 --> 00:52:44,450
Too many poor decisions led to his dismissal.

1399
00:52:44,950 --> 00:52:46,580
Highlights negative consequences.

1400
00:52:46,600 --> 00:52:46,700
Okay.

1401
00:52:46,720 --> 00:52:47,460
And the process.

1402
00:52:47,640 --> 00:52:48,160
Or the person.

1403
00:52:48,460 --> 00:52:51,040
To come to a decision means to decide after thinking.

1404
00:52:51,840 --> 00:52:53,260
He hasn't come to a decision yet.

1405
00:52:53,800 --> 00:52:54,910
Implies deliberation.

1406
00:52:55,470 --> 00:52:58,970
And a key decision maker is the person with authority for those important choices.

1407
00:52:59,610 --> 00:53:01,820
She's the key decision maker on budgets.

1408
00:53:02,300 --> 00:53:03,760
identifies who has the power.

1409
00:53:03,910 --> 00:53:05,390
That clarifies roles nicely.

1410
00:53:06,150 --> 00:53:08,590
What about describing decision-making styles?

1411
00:53:08,990 --> 00:53:10,180
Adjectives are useful here.

1412
00:53:10,780 --> 00:53:13,600
Someone decisive makes decisions quickly, effectively.

1413
00:53:14,200 --> 00:53:17,270
Leaders need to be decisive, seen as a strength.

1414
00:53:17,450 --> 00:53:18,430
Efficient, confident.

1415
00:53:18,490 --> 00:53:19,990
The opposite is indecisive.

1416
00:53:20,670 --> 00:53:26,400
Unable to decide quickly, he's so indecisive it slows everything down, seen as a weakness in many contexts.

1417
00:53:26,380 --> 00:53:27,400
Clear contrast.

1418
00:53:27,540 --> 00:53:31,350
But what if you make a decision and then regret it or rethink?

1419
00:53:31,370 --> 00:53:31,890
Very human.

1420
00:53:32,270 --> 00:53:33,090
You have second thoughts.

1421
00:53:33,640 --> 00:53:34,960
Feeling unsure after deciding.

1422
00:53:35,440 --> 00:53:37,440
She accepted the job but is having second thoughts.

1423
00:53:37,720 --> 00:53:38,420
Reconsidering.

1424
00:53:38,500 --> 00:53:39,180
Doubt creeping in.

1425
00:53:39,240 --> 00:53:41,350
Or you change your mind, altering your decision.

1426
00:53:41,790 --> 00:53:43,210
He agreed but then changed his mind.

1427
00:53:43,350 --> 00:53:46,840
Shows flexibility or sometimes inconsistency depending on context.

1428
00:53:46,820 --> 00:53:47,120
Right.

1429
00:53:47,200 --> 00:53:47,780
And delegates.

1430
00:53:47,900 --> 00:53:51,240
You say it's up to you, meaning the decision is yours.

1431
00:53:51,690 --> 00:53:53,330
Thursday or Friday, it's up to you.

1432
00:53:53,370 --> 00:53:55,590
Or we've briefed Maria.

1433
00:53:55,790 --> 00:53:56,590
Now it's up to her.

1434
00:53:57,250 --> 00:53:59,100
Delegates responsibility clearly.

1435
00:53:59,260 --> 00:54:00,280
Empowers the other person.

1436
00:54:00,500 --> 00:54:00,820
Great.

1437
00:54:01,320 --> 00:54:02,760
OK, decisions covered.

1438
00:54:03,340 --> 00:54:08,250
Now, a potentially sensitive but vital area, salary and pay.

1439
00:54:08,450 --> 00:54:08,670
Yes.

1440
00:54:08,990 --> 00:54:09,950
Crucial for everyone.

1441
00:54:10,490 --> 00:54:14,360
Understanding the terms helps navigate job offers, reviews and personal finance.

1442
00:54:14,430 --> 00:54:15,910
Let's start with the basics.

1443
00:54:15,930 --> 00:54:17,190
Gross versus net pay.

1444
00:54:17,610 --> 00:54:17,850
OK.

1445
00:54:18,310 --> 00:54:21,350
Gross salary pay is your total earnings before anything is taken out.

1446
00:54:21,880 --> 00:54:23,080
The headline number in an offer.

1447
00:54:23,300 --> 00:54:23,880
The big number.

1448
00:54:24,140 --> 00:54:24,300
Right.

1449
00:54:24,620 --> 00:54:28,920
Then come to deductions, tax, insurance, pension contributions, money saved for retirement.

1450
00:54:29,570 --> 00:54:32,030
What's left after deductions is your net salary pay.

1451
00:54:32,170 --> 00:54:33,630
The amount that actually hits your bank account.

1452
00:54:33,670 --> 00:54:34,150
Exactly.

1453
00:54:34,170 --> 00:54:37,390
So gross salary is 50K, but after deductions, the net is lower.

1454
00:54:37,380 --> 00:54:39,540
And take-home pay is just another term for net pay.

1455
00:54:39,560 --> 00:54:39,920
Correct.

1456
00:54:39,940 --> 00:54:41,320
Take-home or take-home pay.

1457
00:54:41,350 --> 00:54:43,890
My take-home pay is about $3,000 a month.

1458
00:54:43,910 --> 00:54:45,780
Very common and formal term for net earnings.

1459
00:54:45,860 --> 00:54:46,080
Okay.

1460
00:54:46,420 --> 00:54:47,160
How about getting more?

1461
00:54:47,740 --> 00:54:48,680
Asking for a raise.

1462
00:54:48,780 --> 00:54:53,150
You ask for a pay rise, UK, or pay raise, US, an increase in salary.

1463
00:54:53,290 --> 00:54:54,550
I often discussed when.

1464
00:54:54,870 --> 00:55:02,620
Usually during a performance appraisal or performance review, that meeting with your boss about your work, you might prepare a self-evaluation for it too.

1465
00:55:02,840 --> 00:55:04,500
Right, linking pay to performance.

1466
00:55:04,700 --> 00:55:07,070
What about extra hours or other earnings?

1467
00:55:07,090 --> 00:55:14,440
Working extra is overtime, sometimes paid, sometimes you get time off in lieu, toe aisle, time off later instead of cash.

1468
00:55:14,420 --> 00:55:15,680
Flexible compensation.

1469
00:55:15,840 --> 00:55:16,060
Yes.

1470
00:55:16,700 --> 00:55:17,610
Then perceptions.

1471
00:55:18,070 --> 00:55:18,510
Underpaid.

1472
00:55:19,070 --> 00:55:19,810
Not enough.

1473
00:55:19,830 --> 00:55:20,310
Well paid.

1474
00:55:20,370 --> 00:55:20,670
A lot.

1475
00:55:21,180 --> 00:55:21,640
Overpaid.

1476
00:55:21,720 --> 00:55:22,140
Too much.

1477
00:55:22,600 --> 00:55:23,580
Often subjective.

1478
00:55:24,300 --> 00:55:25,170
He feels underpaid.

1479
00:55:25,210 --> 00:55:26,170
It's a well-paid job.

1480
00:55:26,690 --> 00:55:28,090
Some think CEOs are overpaid.

1481
00:55:28,190 --> 00:55:31,240
Reflects fairness judgments and non-salary income.

1482
00:55:31,280 --> 00:55:38,930
Salespeople often earn commission, a percentage of sales, 10% commission on sales, and many get a bonus, extra pay, often tied to targets.

1483
00:55:39,290 --> 00:55:41,640
We get an annual bonus if the company hits its goals.

1484
00:55:41,700 --> 00:55:44,400
Performance incentives and the extras.

1485
00:55:44,720 --> 00:55:46,040
Free coffee, gym membership.

1486
00:55:46,020 --> 00:55:46,980
Those are perks.

1487
00:55:47,000 --> 00:55:53,150
Non-salary benefits to attract staff, health insurance, company car, free snacks, part of the total compensation package.

1488
00:55:53,170 --> 00:55:55,460
OK, that really breaks down the whole compensation picture.

1489
00:55:55,520 --> 00:55:57,980
Now, related to career growth experience.

1490
00:55:58,260 --> 00:55:58,980
Equally crucial.

1491
00:55:59,800 --> 00:56:02,830
How you articulate your experience shapes your professional story.

1492
00:56:03,410 --> 00:56:05,450
So different types of experience.

1493
00:56:05,530 --> 00:56:06,610
What's hands on?

1494
00:56:06,640 --> 00:56:11,550
Practical experience or hands-on experience is gained by doing, not just theory.

1495
00:56:11,770 --> 00:56:14,240
I got lots of hands-on experience managing projects.

1496
00:56:14,720 --> 00:56:15,560
Highly valued.

1497
00:56:15,700 --> 00:56:16,740
Real-world learning.

1498
00:56:16,920 --> 00:56:17,160
Yes.

1499
00:56:17,910 --> 00:56:21,290
Similar is firsthand experience gained by doing it yourself directly.

1500
00:56:21,670 --> 00:56:25,800
She has firsthand experience of crisis management, stresses personal involvement.

1501
00:56:25,850 --> 00:56:28,770
Okay, and the flip side, not enough experience.

1502
00:56:28,790 --> 00:56:31,650
That's a lack of experience, often an issue for entry-level roles.

1503
00:56:32,390 --> 00:56:34,620
He lacked the sales experience required.

1504
00:56:34,700 --> 00:56:37,060
A barrier sometimes, and having lots of experience.

1505
00:56:37,080 --> 00:56:40,100
You have a wealth of experience, lots of good, useful background.

1506
00:56:40,120 --> 00:56:43,570
She brings a wealth of experience in marketing, conveys deep knowledge.

1507
00:56:43,590 --> 00:56:46,390
Sounds impressive, and just generally useful experience.

1508
00:56:46,410 --> 00:56:48,630
That's valuable experience, helpful, relevant.

1509
00:56:48,870 --> 00:56:51,930
The internship provided a valuable experience, highlights its utility.

1510
00:56:51,950 --> 00:56:52,890
You can combine them, too.

1511
00:56:53,460 --> 00:56:54,660
Valuable first-hand experience.

1512
00:56:54,640 --> 00:56:55,380
That's impact.

1513
00:56:55,760 --> 00:56:57,140
Okay, experience types covered.

1514
00:56:57,680 --> 00:56:59,480
What about past performance?

1515
00:56:59,500 --> 00:57:00,120
Track records.

1516
00:57:00,930 --> 00:57:02,070
Ah, the track record.

1517
00:57:02,670 --> 00:57:03,710
Hugely important.

1518
00:57:03,930 --> 00:57:05,170
It's your history of performance.

1519
00:57:05,570 --> 00:57:07,110
How well you've done things in the past.

1520
00:57:07,130 --> 00:57:09,030
Your reputation based on results.

1521
00:57:09,050 --> 00:57:09,470
Exactly.

1522
00:57:09,880 --> 00:57:12,560
A good track record means consistent, positive performance.

1523
00:57:13,040 --> 00:57:15,120
He has a good track record in meeting deadlines.

1524
00:57:15,740 --> 00:57:16,400
Reliable.

1525
00:57:16,720 --> 00:57:17,720
Someone dependable.

1526
00:57:17,700 --> 00:57:19,690
An impressive track record is even better.

1527
00:57:20,090 --> 00:57:21,430
Exceptionally good performance.

1528
00:57:21,810 --> 00:57:24,650
An impressive track record of innovation stands out.

1529
00:57:24,750 --> 00:57:26,110
Really notable achievements.

1530
00:57:26,270 --> 00:57:30,420
And a proven track record means shown many times to be very good, reliable.

1531
00:57:30,820 --> 00:57:32,960
A proven track record in turning companies around.

1532
00:57:33,780 --> 00:57:34,660
High level of trust.

1533
00:57:35,060 --> 00:57:36,530
The gold standard almost.

1534
00:57:36,820 --> 00:57:37,750
And the negative.

1535
00:57:37,770 --> 00:57:38,870
A poor track record.

1536
00:57:39,090 --> 00:57:40,430
Often done things badly.

1537
00:57:40,790 --> 00:57:42,230
A poor track record in safety.

1538
00:57:42,910 --> 00:57:43,530
A red flag.

1539
00:57:43,930 --> 00:57:47,260
Track records are how we judge reliability and predict future success.

1540
00:57:47,570 --> 00:57:47,910
Wow.

1541
00:57:48,030 --> 00:57:48,330
Okay.

1542
00:57:48,410 --> 00:57:56,530
We have covered so much ground today, from team talk and meeting mastery to opinions, problems, decisions, pay, experience.

1543
00:57:56,610 --> 00:57:57,790
It's a huge toolkit.

1544
00:57:57,850 --> 00:57:58,330
It really is.

1545
00:57:58,490 --> 00:58:01,010
And mastering it isn't just about sounding good.

1546
00:58:01,200 --> 00:58:07,510
No, it's about being effective, navigating work confidently, clearly, making an impact.

1547
00:58:07,490 --> 00:58:19,640
If we connect this to the bigger picture, knowing this language is honestly a shortcut, a shortcut to feeling more capable, more informed, more confident in almost any professional situation you'll face.

1548
00:58:19,750 --> 00:58:25,860
It empowers you to participate fully, influence things, and really drive your career forward.

1549
00:58:25,900 --> 00:58:26,420
Precisely.

1550
00:58:26,720 --> 00:58:30,750
So the challenge for you listening is don't just listen.

1551
00:58:30,790 --> 00:58:32,390
Try to use these phrases.

1552
00:58:32,570 --> 00:58:34,530
Pick a couple from each section that resonate.

1553
00:58:34,930 --> 00:58:35,920
Try them out this week.

1554
00:58:36,080 --> 00:58:36,760
See how they feel.

1555
00:58:36,860 --> 00:58:38,380
See how they change your interactions.

1556
00:58:38,440 --> 00:58:39,040
Start small.

1557
00:58:39,120 --> 00:58:40,020
Build confidence.

1558
00:58:40,140 --> 00:58:40,680
Exactly.

1559
00:58:40,740 --> 00:58:42,400
This raises an important question.

1560
00:58:42,990 --> 00:58:45,830
Well, with everything changing so fast,

1561
00:58:45,810 --> 00:58:49,620
global business, remote work, AI impacting communication.

1562
00:58:50,380 --> 00:58:54,230
How do you think our idea of professional and effective communication will shift?

1563
00:58:54,810 --> 00:58:59,960
What new essential vocabulary might we need in, say, five or 10 years to really stay ahead?

1564
00:59:00,480 --> 00:59:06,670
How will we talk about collaboration or decisions when so much more is digital or even automated?

1565
00:59:06,840 --> 00:59:08,000
That is a fascinating question.

1566
00:59:08,080 --> 00:59:10,760
What will be the essential business English of the future?

1567
00:59:10,840 --> 00:59:11,980
Something definitely to think about.

1568
00:59:12,100 --> 00:59:12,660
Food for thought?

1569
00:59:13,000 --> 00:59:13,580
Indeed.

1570
00:59:14,120 --> 00:59:16,580
Well, thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

1571
00:59:16,640 --> 00:59:18,140
It's been incredibly insightful.

1572
00:59:18,410 --> 00:59:18,870
My pleasure.

1573
00:59:19,270 --> 00:59:20,590
And thank you all for listening.

1574
00:59:20,750 --> 00:59:36,300
We look forward to our next exploration together on the deep dive.
